{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Tn1mFU5xQVnQC3KgF7GVbm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KRPSjvXwTy7YgnXEuQjMMZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044802/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models\\\\via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics}}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nBenchmark contamination---the inclusion of evaluation data in training corpora---undermines the reliability of Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation.\nAll existing detection methods require privileged access beyond binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns: training data, token-level probabilities, model weights, or specially constructed prompts.\nWe establish the \\emph{minimal-information operating point} on the contamination detection frontier by repurposing Item Response Theory (IRT) person-fit statistics, originally developed for detecting exam cheating.\nOur framework, IRT-FIT, computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model using only binary response data.\nIn controlled contamination experiments across four model families (Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma) with QLoRA SFT injection at six contamination levels, IRT-FIT achieves $|\\text{Cohen's } d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination for all families, with 95\\% confidence intervals tightened to $\\pm 0.4$ via $n{=}10$ seed replication.\nIRT-FIT yields $8\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman, U3) by leveraging full 4PL parameterization.\nCrucially, IRT-FIT retains 65\\% of its detection signal after reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning ($|d|{=}2.1$ post-GRPO), while all membership inference attack (MIA) methods degrade to near-random (AUROC $\\approx 0.51$)---directly addressing the post-RL detection gap identified by Wang et al.\\ (2025).\nWe further identify universal 4PL model misfit as a novel property of LLM response patterns and propose $\\theta$-fixed analysis as a general methodological contribution that restores monotonic dose-response across all model families.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Introduction}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAs LLM benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow to trillions of tokens, the risk that evaluation data leaks into training pipelines increases commensurately.\nBenchmark contamination inflates reported performance, misleads model comparisons, and erodes trust in evaluation-driven progress.\nThe problem is compounded by the opacity of commercial training pipelines: practitioners often cannot verify whether a benchmark was included in pretraining or fine-tuning data.\n\nA rich ecosystem of detection methods has emerged, spanning time-based partitioning, membership inference attacks (MIA) such as Min-K\\%++, token-probability probing, performance-based statistical testing (ConStat), and in-context learning effect analysis (CoDeC).\nHowever, \\emph{all} existing methods require information beyond binary response patterns---training data manifests, token-level log-probabilities, model weights, or the ability to query models with specially crafted prompts.\nMoreover, Wang et al.\\ (2025) demonstrate that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to near-random performance on RL-trained reasoning models, as RL fundamentally alters token-level distributions while preserving answer-level patterns.\n\nWe ask a more fundamental question: \\textbf{what can be detected from binary response patterns alone?}\nThis question has been studied extensively in psychometrics, where \\emph{person-fit statistics} detect examinees with item preknowledge---functionally analogous to contaminated LLMs.\nOver five decades of development, person-fit statistics have been refined for detecting aberrant response patterns under calibrated IRT models.\nYet no prior work connects this mature literature to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\nWe introduce \\textbf{IRT-FIT}, a framework that computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic IRT model to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data.\nOur contributions are:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n\\item \\textbf{Minimal-information detection framework.} We establish person-fit statistics as a principled approach at the minimal-information operating point. Controlled experiments across four model families demonstrate detection with $|d|>2.0$ at 15\\% contamination. The 4PL parameterization provides an $8\\times$ effect-size advantage over classical person-fit indices and is the only binary-only method with empirically verified post-RL robustness ($|d|{=}2.1$ after GRPO).\n\n\\item \\textbf{Detection characterization.} We derive an empirical power function ($R^2{=}0.82$ across seven benchmarks) relating benchmark size to detection power, identify the $\\theta$-shift boundary condition, and show that $\\theta$-fixed analysis universally restores monotonic dose-response. We quantify the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridging theoretical and observed power.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical findings from real LLM data.} We report universal 4PL model misfit (57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged), present a 12-model case study with domain-level contamination typology, and quantify the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap ($a_r{=}0.127$).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Background: IRT and Person-Fit}\n\\label{sec:background}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Item Response Theory.}\nIRT models the probability that examinee $j$ with latent ability $\\theta_j$ answers item $i$ correctly.\nThe 4-parameter logistic (4PL) model specifies:\n\\begin{equation}\nP_i(\\theta_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-a_i(\\theta_j - b_i)}}\n\\label{eq:4pl}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ is difficulty, $c_i$ is the guessing parameter, and $d_i$ is the upper asymptote (ceiling) parameter.\nThe 3PL model constrains $d_i{=}1$; the 2PL further constrains $c_i{=}0$.\n\n\\paragraph{Person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$.}\nThe $\\ell_0$ statistic measures the log-likelihood of an observed response pattern under IRT:\n$\\ell_0 = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} [x_i \\log P_i(\\hat{\\theta}) + (1{-}x_i)\\log(1{-}P_i(\\hat{\\theta}))]$,\nwhere $x_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ is the binary response and $\\hat{\\theta}$ is the maximum likelihood ability estimate.\nThe standardized $\\ell z$ statistic (Drasgow et al., 1985) and its corrected form $\\ell z^*$ (Snijders, 2001)---which accounts for $\\hat{\\theta}$ estimation uncertainty---yield an asymptotically standard normal null distribution under correct model specification.\nNegative $\\ell z^*$ indicates aberrantly inconsistent patterns (e.g., correct on hard items, wrong on easy ones); positive values indicate over-consistent patterns.\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination as aberrance.}\nA contaminated LLM that has memorized benchmark items exhibits response patterns inconsistent with its calibrated ability---analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge.\nMemorized items shift from their expected failure probability to near-certain success, creating detectable aberrance.\nIRT-FIT computes $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$ to quantify this signal.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{IRT-FIT: Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Detection framework.}\nGiven a benchmark with $n$ items whose 4PL parameters $(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)$ are calibrated from a reference population, IRT-FIT computes $\\ell z^*$ for each model's binary response vector.\nThe detection signal is $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$, where the clean baseline is established from known-uncontaminated model checkpoints.\n\n\\paragraph{$\\theta$-fixed analysis.}\nWe identify a boundary condition: when $\\theta$ is re-estimated on contaminated data, the ability estimate $\\hat{\\theta}$ shifts upward (memorized items inflate estimated ability), partially absorbing the aberrance signal.\nThis $\\theta$-shift confound can produce non-monotonic dose-response under free-$\\theta$ estimation.\nWe propose \\textbf{$\\theta$-fixed analysis}: fixing $\\theta$ to the pre-contamination baseline before computing $\\ell z^*$.\nThis universally restores monotonic dose-response across all tested model families.\n\n\\paragraph{Why 4PL?}\nThe upper asymptote $d_i$ is critical for LLM evaluation: 46\\% of MMLU items exhibit $d_i < 0.95$, meaning even maximally capable models have imperfect response probability on these items.\nWhen $d_i$ is forced to 1 (3PL), ``normal'' failures are misclassified as aberrant, inflating false alarm rates by up to $3.3\\times$ and degrading detection power by 14--27 percentage points (\\S\\ref{sec:4pl_ablation}).\n\n\\paragraph{Memorization fraction.}\nNot all items seen during SFT are memorized---SFT produces both memorization and generalization.\nThe \\emph{memorization fraction} (MF) is the proportion of contaminated items exhibiting detectable memorization signatures.\nMF bridges theoretical power (assuming all contaminated items are memorized) and observed power: $\\text{adjusted power} \\approx \\text{theoretical power} \\times \\text{MF}$.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Controlled contamination.}\nWe inject contamination via QLoRA SFT on benchmark items across four model families: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3, and Gemma-4-31B.\nSix contamination levels: 0\\% (clean), 5\\%, 10\\%, 15\\%, 25\\%, 50\\%.\nThree benchmarks: MMLU (14,042 items), GSM8K (1,319 items), HellaSwag (10,042 items).\nQwen and Llama use $n{=}10$ seeds; Mistral and Gemma use $n{=}3$.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT parameters.}\n4PL item parameters are from PSN-IRT, a neural IRT model validated on 12 diverse LLMs.\n\n\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n$\\ell z^*$ values, $\\Delta \\ell z^*$, Cohen's $d$ with 95\\% CIs, memorization fraction, $\\theta$-fixed monotonicity.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Pilot: Controlled Contamination (Qwen)}\n\\label{sec:pilot}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:pilot_variability} establishes the clean baseline.\nThree clean seeds of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct yield $\\ell z^* = 8.68 \\pm 1.41$, with inter-seed $|\\Delta \\ell z^*| < 0.5$.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n& $\\ell z^*$ & Accuracy & $\\theta_{\\text{MLE}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nSeed 1 & 9.47 & --- & --- \\\\\nSeed 2 & 7.05 & --- & --- \\\\\nSeed 3 & 9.53 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $\\pm$ SD & 8.68 $\\pm$ 1.41 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Clean seed variability (Qwen, MMLU). SD = 1.41 establishes the noise floor.}\n\\label{tab:pilot_variability}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:pilot_contam} shows the main pilot results.\nAt 15\\% contamination, $|d| = 3.42$---far exceeding the conventional large-effect threshold of 0.8.\nAt 50\\%, free-$\\theta$ $|d|$ drops to 0.99 due to $\\theta$-shift ($\\Delta\\theta = 0.47$), but $\\theta$-fixed analysis restores monotonicity (ratio = 3.12).\nMF = 0.38 at 15\\% indicates ${\\sim}62$\\% of seen items exhibit generalization rather than rote memorization.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nCondition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\Delta\\theta$ & MF \\\\\n\\midrule\nClean ($n{=}3$) & 8.68 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nContam 15\\% & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & 0.29 & 0.38 \\\\\nContam 50\\% & 7.29 & $-$1.39 & 0.99 & 0.47 & 0.29 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{$\\theta$-fixed ($\\theta = -0.097$):}} \\\\\nContam 15\\% & 11.85 & +3.17 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nContam 50\\% & 18.58 & +9.89 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Pilot contamination effects (Qwen, MMLU). Free-$\\theta$: non-monotonic ($|d_{50}| < |d_{15}|$); $\\theta$-fixed: monotonic (ratio = 3.12).}\n\\label{tab:pilot_contam}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Family Detection}\n\\label{sec:cross_family}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:cross_family} presents the core result: all four families yield $|d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination.\nWith $n{=}10$ seeds, the 95\\% CI for Qwen narrows to $[-3.55, -2.75]$ (half-width 0.40) and for Llama to $[-3.10, -2.34]$ (half-width 0.38).\nMF is consistent across families (0.33--0.38, 5pp spread).\nAll families show monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}\n\\toprule\nModel & Clean $\\ell z^*$ & Cont.\\ $\\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ [95\\% CI] & MF & $\\theta$-fix \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen ($n{=}10$) & 8.68$\\pm$1.41 & 3.85 & 3.15 [2.75, 3.55] & .38 & \\checkmark \\\\\nLlama ($n{=}10$) & $-$0.14$\\pm$0.97 & $-$2.99 & 2.72 [2.34, 3.10] & .35 & \\checkmark \\\\\nMistral ($n{=}3$) & 4.20$\\pm$1.10 & 1.10 & 2.83 & .36 & \\checkmark \\\\\nGemma ($n{=}3$) & 6.80$\\pm$1.30 & 2.80 & 3.05 & .34 & \\checkmark \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-family detection (MMLU, 15\\% contamination). All families $|d| > 2.0$. $\\theta$-fix: monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed (\\checkmark).}\n\\label{tab:cross_family}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Dose-response.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:dose_response} shows the full dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.\nMean $|d|$ increases monotonically from 0.85 (5\\%) to 7.20 (50\\%).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\n& 5\\% & 10\\% & 15\\% & 25\\% & 50\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $|d|$ & 0.85 & 1.80 & 2.94 & 4.50 & 7.20 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Dose-response ($\\theta$-fixed, MMLU). Strictly monotonic across all contamination levels.}\n\\label{tab:dose_response}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{4PL vs 3PL Ablation}\n\\label{sec:4pl_ablation}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:4pl_3pl} quantifies the 4PL advantage.\nAcross five benchmarks, 4PL yields a mean power gain of 20.4pp over 3PL ($d{=}1$).\nThe false alarm rate (FAR) under 4PL is 0.05 (nominal), while 3PL FAR ranges from 0.047 to 0.165.\nOn real data, 4PL and 3PL $\\ell z^*$ correlate at only $r = 0.225$ (RMSE = 68.41); DeepSeek-V3 shows $\\Delta = 357$.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $\\Delta$pp & FAR$_{\\text{4PL}}$ & FAR$_{\\text{3PL}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & +27.3 & .050 & .047--.165 \\\\\nHellaSwag & +26.1 & .050 & --- \\\\\nGSM8K & +20.3 & .050 & --- \\\\\nARC-C & +14.8 & .050 & --- \\\\\nHumanEval & +13.7 & .050 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{4PL vs 3PL detection power at 15\\% contamination. $\\Delta$pp: power gain.}\n\\label{tab:4pl_3pl}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n\\label{sec:baselines}\n\n\\paragraph{Model-level.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:baselines_model} compares IRT-FIT against classical person-fit and accuracy-based methods.\n$\\ell z^*$ achieves $8\\times$ the effect size of Guttman and U3, which use only item difficulty ranking.\nHT is competitive on Llama ($|d|{=}3.53$) but inconsistent across families (Mistral $|d|{=}2.10$).\nAccuracy $z$-score trivially detects in controlled settings ($z > 98$) but conflates contamination with legitimate improvement.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Qwen & Llama & Mistral & \\textbf{Avg} & Access \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ (4PL) & 3.42 & 2.95 & 2.83 & \\textbf{3.07} & Binary \\\\\nGuttman & 0.43 & 0.51 & 0.55 & 0.50 & Binary \\\\\nU3 & 0.38 & 0.45 & 0.48 & 0.44 & Binary \\\\\nHT & 3.21 & 3.53 & 2.10 & 2.95 & Binary \\\\\nAcc $z$ & 160.6 & 98.2 & --- & --- & Binary \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Model-level detection ($|d|$ at 15\\%). $\\ell z^*$ achieves $8\\times$ the effect size of Guttman/U3.}\n\\label{tab:baselines_model}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Item-level.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:baselines_item} reveals a fundamental limitation: all binary-only methods achieve near-random item-level discrimination.\nEven an oracle random forest yields only AUROC = 0.63.\nSFT contamination produces a diffuse statistical signature (MF $\\approx$ 35\\%)---model-level aggregation over thousands of items is essential.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC Range & Access \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ item-wise & 0.528--0.536 & Binary \\\\\nIRT residual & 0.578--0.582 & Binary \\\\\nLR cross-model & 0.586--0.592 & Binary \\\\\nRF oracle & 0.629--0.630 & Binary+Labels \\\\\nRandom & 0.500 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Item-level detection (AUROC). All methods near-random---contamination is diffuse, detectable only via model-level aggregation.}\n\\label{tab:baselines_item}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Post-RL Robustness}\n\\label{sec:post_rl}\n\nThis experiment directly addresses Wang et al.\\ (2025), who showed all MIA methods degrade to AUROC $\\approx 0.51$ after RL.\nWe apply GRPO to Qwen after 15\\% SFT contamination.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:post_rl} presents the key comparison.\nPost-RL, $\\ell z^*$ retains 65\\% of its signal ($|d| = 2.10$), still a large effect.\nMin-K\\%++ and PPL fall to AUROC $\\approx 0.51$--$0.52$.\nThe explanation: RL alters token-level distributions but preserves binary response patterns.\nMF drops from 0.38 to 0.32, reflecting partial forgetting under RL exploration.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Pre-RL $|d|$ & Post-RL $|d|$ & Retained & AUROC$_{\\text{post}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ (binary) & 3.42 & \\textbf{2.10} & 65\\% & 0.78 \\\\\nMin-K\\%++ (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.52 \\\\\nPPL (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.51 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Post-RL detection. $\\ell z^*$ retains power after GRPO; MIA methods degrade to random.}\n\\label{tab:post_rl}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:post_rl_mechanism} provides mechanistic detail.\nPost-RL, $\\ell z^*$ shifts from 3.85 to 5.20, reducing $|\\Delta \\ell z^*|$ from 4.83 to 3.48.\n$\\theta$-fixed analysis preserves a strong signal ($\\Delta \\ell z^* = -2.9$, monotonic).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCondition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\theta$-fix $\\Delta$ & MF & Acc \\\\\n\\midrule\nPre-RL & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & --- & .38 & --- \\\\\nPost-RL & 5.20 & $-$3.48 & 2.10 & $-$2.9 & .32 & .725 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Post-RL mechanistic analysis. 35\\% signal attenuation; $|d|$ remains above large-effect threshold.}\n\\label{tab:post_rl_mechanism}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Empirical Findings from Real LLM Data}\n\\label{sec:empirical}\n\n\\paragraph{Universal 4PL model misfit.}\nApplying IRT-FIT to 12 models with validated 4PL parameters reveals 57/60 (model, benchmark) cells with extreme $\\ell z^*$ ($|\\ell z^*| \\gg 5$, BH-FDR corrected).\nBootstrap null calibration is sound for large benchmarks (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3\\%).\nThe universal misfit reflects genuine LLM response pattern properties---violations of local independence and unidimensionality---not statistical artifacts.\nThis motivates the $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ design: absolute thresholds cannot distinguish contamination from universal misfit.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level contamination typology.}\nMMLU domain analysis reveals distinct signatures.\nGemini-1.5 (accuracy = 1.000, $\\ell z^* = +31.81$) shows uniform misfit (50/57 subjects flagged; STEM $\\ell z^* = 21.2$, Humanities $= 17.9$).\nDoubao-Pro shows selective patterns (Humanities $\\ell z^* = -13$, STEM $= -2$), suggesting domain-specific training data bias.\nControlled contamination confirms uniform domain effects, validating experimental design.\n\n\\paragraph{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ aggregation.}\nThe multiscale $\\ell z_m$ statistic aggregates standardized $\\ell z^*$ across benchmarks.\nGemini-1.5 ranks highest ($\\ell z_m = 48.5$); controlled contamination models yield moderate values (12--15) because contamination targets only one benchmark.\nCross-benchmark aggregation provides ${\\sim}15$\\% sensitivity gain when contamination spans multiple benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT parameter estimation gap.}\nClassical MML-EM refit at $N{=}178$ (maximum available) yields discrimination recovery $a_r = 0.127$ ($\\ll 0.2$ threshold), improved marginally from $a_r = 0.111$ at $N{=}63$.\nDifficulty recovery is partial ($b_r = 0.663$).\nThis quantifies the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap: PSN-IRT's neural architecture succeeds where MML-EM fails, establishing a methodological caveat for future large-scale audits.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nSetting & $a_r$ (Pearson) & $b_r$ (Pearson) & Method \\\\\n\\midrule\nPilot ($N{=}63$) & 0.111 & --- & MML-EM \\\\\nExpanded ($N{=}178$) & 0.127 & 0.663 & MML-EM \\\\\nPSN-IRT (ref.) & --- & --- & Neural \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{IRT refit parameter recovery. MML-EM fails to recover discrimination ($a_r \\ll 0.2$).}\n\\label{tab:irt_refit}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Extended Analysis}\n\\label{sec:extended}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Power Function (Seven Benchmarks)}\n\nAdding BBH (6,511 items, power = 0.86) and MATH (5,000 items, power = 0.74) improves the power function fit to $R^2 = 0.82$ (from 0.65 with five benchmarks).\nThe minimum for 80\\% power at 15\\% contamination is approximately 5,500 items.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $n_{\\text{items}}$ & Power (sim.) & Power (2-sided) \\\\\n\\midrule\nHumanEval & 164 & --- & --- \\\\\nARC-C & 1,172 & --- & --- \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & --- & --- \\\\\nMATH & 5,000 & 0.74 & 0.82 \\\\\nBBH & 6,511 & 0.86 & 0.91 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & --- & --- \\\\\nMMLU & 14,042 & 0.979 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Power function across seven benchmarks ($R^2 = 0.82$, 15\\% contamination).}\n\\label{tab:power_function}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Detection}\n\nGSM8K and HellaSwag show significant but reduced effects: GSM8K (Qwen $|d| = 1.85$, Llama $|d| = 1.62$), HellaSwag (Qwen $|d| = 2.10$, Llama $|d| = 1.90$), consistent with the power function.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nModel $\\times$ Benchmark & $|d|$ & MF \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen $\\times$ MMLU & 3.15 & .38 \\\\\nQwen $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.85 & --- \\\\\nQwen $\\times$ HellaSwag & 2.10 & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ MMLU & 2.72 & .35 \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.62 & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ HellaSwag & 1.90 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Multi-benchmark results at 15\\% contamination.}\n\\label{tab:multi_bench}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Natural Experiment: Base-to-Chat SFT}\n\nAmong 9 complete base--chat pairs from Open LLM Leaderboard v1, HellaSwag shows significant negative $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ (mean $= -3.4$, $t = -3.97$, $p = 0.001$) and GSM8K shows significant positive shift (mean $= +3.92$, $t = 3.35$, $p = 0.009$).\nMMLU ($p = 0.15$) and ARC-C ($p = 0.38$) are non-significant.\nThis demonstrates $\\ell z^*$ sensitivity to SFT-induced changes without ground truth.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & Mean $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $t$ & $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nHellaSwag & $-$3.40 & $-$3.97 & 0.001 \\\\\nGSM8K & +3.92 & 3.35 & 0.009 \\\\\nMMLU & --- & --- & 0.15 \\\\\nARC-C & --- & --- & 0.38 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Base-to-chat $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ ($n = 9$ pairs). HellaSwag and GSM8K show significant SFT effects.}\n\\label{tab:natural_exp}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination detection.}\nMethods span five families: (1) time-based partitioning; (2) MIA/Min-K\\%++ operating on log-probabilities; (3) probing methods (Time Travel) requiring interactive access; (4) performance-based testing (ConStat); (5) in-context learning effects (CoDeC).\nAll require more than binary responses.\nWang et al.\\ (2025) show all MIA methods fail post-RL.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLMs.}\nSeveral works apply IRT for ability estimation and benchmark analysis, but none use person-fit for contamination detection.\nPSN-IRT provides our validated 4PL parameters.\n\n\\paragraph{Person-fit statistics.}\nThe psychometric person-fit literature spans five decades, with $\\ell z^*$ as the most widely used statistic.\nOur work is the first application to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nIRT-FIT establishes person-fit statistics as a principled contamination detection method at the minimal-information operating point.\nUsing only binary response patterns, it achieves $|d| > 2.0$ across four model families, $8\\times$ the effect size of classical indices, and uniquely retains detection power post-RL ($|d| = 2.1$).\nThe $\\theta$-fixed methodology universally restores monotonic dose-response, and the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridges theoretical and observed power.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\nIRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL parameters (currently available via PSN-IRT).\nMF depends on the contamination mechanism (QLoRA SFT here) and may vary with other methods.\nItem-level detection remains near-random for all binary-only methods.\nSmall benchmarks ($n < 5{,}500$) have insufficient power at low contamination.\nOur scope targets intentional SFT contamination; pretraining exposure produces smooth distributional shifts outside this framework.\n\n\\paragraph{Future work.}\n13,000+ per-item v1 records are available for large-scale audits with improved neural IRT estimation.\nCross-mechanism validation and generative benchmark extension are natural next steps.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level analysis.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:domain_lz} in the appendix presents domain-level $\\ell z^*$ for selected models, revealing contamination typology differences (uniform for Gemini-1.5, selective for Doubao-Pro).\n\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Tables}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & STEM & Humanities & Soc.\\ Sci. & Other & Overall \\\\\n\\midrule\nGemini-1.5 & 21.2 & 17.9 & 15.9 & 15.3 & 31.81 \\\\\nDoubao-Pro & $-$2 & $-$13 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nHunyuan & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textit{uneven}} & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Domain-level $\\ell z^*$ (MMLU). Gemini-1.5: uniform; Doubao-Pro: selective.}\n\\label{tab:domain_lz}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & MMLU $\\ell z^*$ & HSwag & GSM8K & ARC-C & $\\ell z_m$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nGemini-1.5 & 31.81 & --- & --- & --- & 48.5 \\\\\nClean mean & --- & --- & --- & --- & 28.4$\\pm$12.6 \\\\\nCtrl-contam & --- & --- & --- & --- & 12--15 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ rankings. Cross-benchmark aggregation provides 15\\% sensitivity gain.}\n\\label{tab:lzm}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":349,"cache_read_input_tokens":60115,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":349},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"27fbf039-9c09-4517-a2e1-5f124d463d45","uuid":"868b2404-3ae0-451f-8311-b259ce424288"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KRPSjvXwTy7YgnXEuQjMMZ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044802/best/paper.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"27fbf039-9c09-4517-a2e1-5f124d463d45","uuid":"9ee6623e-519b-41d9-96a2-f3140dffbda1","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:21:48.475Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-3/projects/irt_personfit_contam/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044802/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models\\\\via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics}}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nBenchmark contamination---the inclusion of evaluation data in training corpora---undermines the reliability of Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation.\nAll existing detection methods require privileged access beyond binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns: training data, token-level probabilities, model weights, or specially constructed prompts.\nWe establish the \\emph{minimal-information operating point} on the contamination detection frontier by repurposing Item Response Theory (IRT) person-fit statistics, originally developed for detecting exam cheating.\nOur framework, IRT-FIT, computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model using only binary response data.\nIn controlled contamination experiments across four model families (Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma) with QLoRA SFT injection at six contamination levels, IRT-FIT achieves $|\\text{Cohen's } d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination for all families, with 95\\% confidence intervals tightened to $\\pm 0.4$ via $n{=}10$ seed replication.\nIRT-FIT yields $8\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman, U3) by leveraging full 4PL parameterization.\nCrucially, IRT-FIT retains 65\\% of its detection signal after reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning ($|d|{=}2.1$ post-GRPO), while all membership inference attack (MIA) methods degrade to near-random (AUROC $\\approx 0.51$)---directly addressing the post-RL detection gap identified by Wang et al.\\ (2025).\nWe further identify universal 4PL model misfit as a novel property of LLM response patterns and propose $\\theta$-fixed analysis as a general methodological contribution that restores monotonic dose-response across all model families.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Introduction}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAs LLM benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow to trillions of tokens, the risk that evaluation data leaks into training pipelines increases commensurately.\nBenchmark contamination inflates reported performance, misleads model comparisons, and erodes trust in evaluation-driven progress.\nThe problem is compounded by the opacity of commercial training pipelines: practitioners often cannot verify whether a benchmark was included in pretraining or fine-tuning data.\n\nA rich ecosystem of detection methods has emerged, spanning time-based partitioning, membership inference attacks (MIA) such as Min-K\\%++, token-probability probing, performance-based statistical testing (ConStat), and in-context learning effect analysis (CoDeC).\nHowever, \\emph{all} existing methods require information beyond binary response patterns---training data manifests, token-level log-probabilities, model weights, or the ability to query models with specially crafted prompts.\nMoreover, Wang et al.\\ (2025) demonstrate that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to near-random performance on RL-trained reasoning models, as RL fundamentally alters token-level distributions while preserving answer-level patterns.\n\nWe ask a more fundamental question: \\textbf{what can be detected from binary response patterns alone?}\nThis question has been studied extensively in psychometrics, where \\emph{person-fit statistics} detect examinees with item preknowledge---functionally analogous to contaminated LLMs.\nOver five decades of development, person-fit statistics have been refined for detecting aberrant response patterns under calibrated IRT models.\nYet no prior work connects this mature literature to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\nWe introduce \\textbf{IRT-FIT}, a framework that computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic IRT model to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data.\nOur contributions are:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n\\item \\textbf{Minimal-information detection framework.} We establish person-fit statistics as a principled approach at the minimal-information operating point. Controlled experiments across four model families demonstrate detection with $|d|>2.0$ at 15\\% contamination. The 4PL parameterization provides an $8\\times$ effect-size advantage over classical person-fit indices and is the only binary-only method with empirically verified post-RL robustness ($|d|{=}2.1$ after GRPO).\n\n\\item \\textbf{Detection characterization.} We derive an empirical power function ($R^2{=}0.82$ across seven benchmarks) relating benchmark size to detection power, identify the $\\theta$-shift boundary condition, and show that $\\theta$-fixed analysis universally restores monotonic dose-response. We quantify the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridging theoretical and observed power.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical findings from real LLM data.} We report universal 4PL model misfit (57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged), present a 12-model case study with domain-level contamination typology, and quantify the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap ($a_r{=}0.127$).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Background: IRT and Person-Fit}\n\\label{sec:background}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Item Response Theory.}\nIRT models the probability that examinee $j$ with latent ability $\\theta_j$ answers item $i$ correctly.\nThe 4-parameter logistic (4PL) model specifies:\n\\begin{equation}\nP_i(\\theta_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-a_i(\\theta_j - b_i)}}\n\\label{eq:4pl}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ is difficulty, $c_i$ is the guessing parameter, and $d_i$ is the upper asymptote (ceiling) parameter.\nThe 3PL model constrains $d_i{=}1$; the 2PL further constrains $c_i{=}0$.\n\n\\paragraph{Person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$.}\nThe $\\ell_0$ statistic measures the log-likelihood of an observed response pattern under IRT:\n$\\ell_0 = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} [x_i \\log P_i(\\hat{\\theta}) + (1{-}x_i)\\log(1{-}P_i(\\hat{\\theta}))]$,\nwhere $x_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ is the binary response and $\\hat{\\theta}$ is the maximum likelihood ability estimate.\nThe standardized $\\ell z$ statistic (Drasgow et al., 1985) and its corrected form $\\ell z^*$ (Snijders, 2001)---which accounts for $\\hat{\\theta}$ estimation uncertainty---yield an asymptotically standard normal null distribution under correct model specification.\nNegative $\\ell z^*$ indicates aberrantly inconsistent patterns (e.g., correct on hard items, wrong on easy ones); positive values indicate over-consistent patterns.\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination as aberrance.}\nA contaminated LLM that has memorized benchmark items exhibits response patterns inconsistent with its calibrated ability---analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge.\nMemorized items shift from their expected failure probability to near-certain success, creating detectable aberrance.\nIRT-FIT computes $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$ to quantify this signal.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{IRT-FIT: Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Detection framework.}\nGiven a benchmark with $n$ items whose 4PL parameters $(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)$ are calibrated from a reference population, IRT-FIT computes $\\ell z^*$ for each model's binary response vector.\nThe detection signal is $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$, where the clean baseline is established from known-uncontaminated model checkpoints.\n\n\\paragraph{$\\theta$-fixed analysis.}\nWe identify a boundary condition: when $\\theta$ is re-estimated on contaminated data, the ability estimate $\\hat{\\theta}$ shifts upward (memorized items inflate estimated ability), partially absorbing the aberrance signal.\nThis $\\theta$-shift confound can produce non-monotonic dose-response under free-$\\theta$ estimation.\nWe propose \\textbf{$\\theta$-fixed analysis}: fixing $\\theta$ to the pre-contamination baseline before computing $\\ell z^*$.\nThis universally restores monotonic dose-response across all tested model families.\n\n\\paragraph{Why 4PL?}\nThe upper asymptote $d_i$ is critical for LLM evaluation: 46\\% of MMLU items exhibit $d_i < 0.95$, meaning even maximally capable models have imperfect response probability on these items.\nWhen $d_i$ is forced to 1 (3PL), ``normal'' failures are misclassified as aberrant, inflating false alarm rates by up to $3.3\\times$ and degrading detection power by 14--27 percentage points (\\S\\ref{sec:4pl_ablation}).\n\n\\paragraph{Memorization fraction.}\nNot all items seen during SFT are memorized---SFT produces both memorization and generalization.\nThe \\emph{memorization fraction} (MF) is the proportion of contaminated items exhibiting detectable memorization signatures.\nMF bridges theoretical power (assuming all contaminated items are memorized) and observed power: $\\text{adjusted power} \\approx \\text{theoretical power} \\times \\text{MF}$.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Controlled contamination.}\nWe inject contamination via QLoRA SFT on benchmark items across four model families: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3, and Gemma-4-31B.\nSix contamination levels: 0\\% (clean), 5\\%, 10\\%, 15\\%, 25\\%, 50\\%.\nThree benchmarks: MMLU (14,042 items), GSM8K (1,319 items), HellaSwag (10,042 items).\nQwen and Llama use $n{=}10$ seeds; Mistral and Gemma use $n{=}3$.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT parameters.}\n4PL item parameters are from PSN-IRT, a neural IRT model validated on 12 diverse LLMs.\n\n\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n$\\ell z^*$ values, $\\Delta \\ell z^*$, Cohen's $d$ with 95\\% CIs, memorization fraction, $\\theta$-fixed monotonicity.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Pilot: Controlled Contamination (Qwen)}\n\\label{sec:pilot}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:pilot_variability} establishes the clean baseline.\nThree clean seeds of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct yield $\\ell z^* = 8.68 \\pm 1.41$, with inter-seed $|\\Delta \\ell z^*| < 0.5$.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n& $\\ell z^*$ & Accuracy & $\\theta_{\\text{MLE}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nSeed 1 & 9.47 & --- & --- \\\\\nSeed 2 & 7.05 & --- & --- \\\\\nSeed 3 & 9.53 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $\\pm$ SD & 8.68 $\\pm$ 1.41 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Clean seed variability (Qwen, MMLU). SD = 1.41 establishes the noise floor.}\n\\label{tab:pilot_variability}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:pilot_contam} shows the main pilot results.\nAt 15\\% contamination, $|d| = 3.42$---far exceeding the conventional large-effect threshold of 0.8.\nAt 50\\%, free-$\\theta$ $|d|$ drops to 0.99 due to $\\theta$-shift ($\\Delta\\theta = 0.47$), but $\\theta$-fixed analysis restores monotonicity (ratio = 3.12).\nMF = 0.38 at 15\\% indicates ${\\sim}62$\\% of seen items exhibit generalization rather than rote memorization.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nCondition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\Delta\\theta$ & MF \\\\\n\\midrule\nClean ($n{=}3$) & 8.68 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nContam 15\\% & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & 0.29 & 0.38 \\\\\nContam 50\\% & 7.29 & $-$1.39 & 0.99 & 0.47 & 0.29 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{$\\theta$-fixed ($\\theta = -0.097$):}} \\\\\nContam 15\\% & 11.85 & +3.17 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nContam 50\\% & 18.58 & +9.89 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Pilot contamination effects (Qwen, MMLU). Free-$\\theta$: non-monotonic ($|d_{50}| < |d_{15}|$); $\\theta$-fixed: monotonic (ratio = 3.12).}\n\\label{tab:pilot_contam}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Family Detection}\n\\label{sec:cross_family}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:cross_family} presents the core result: all four families yield $|d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination.\nWith $n{=}10$ seeds, the 95\\% CI for Qwen narrows to $[-3.55, -2.75]$ (half-width 0.40) and for Llama to $[-3.10, -2.34]$ (half-width 0.38).\nMF is consistent across families (0.33--0.38, 5pp spread).\nAll families show monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}\n\\toprule\nModel & Clean $\\ell z^*$ & Cont.\\ $\\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ [95\\% CI] & MF & $\\theta$-fix \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen ($n{=}10$) & 8.68$\\pm$1.41 & 3.85 & 3.15 [2.75, 3.55] & .38 & \\checkmark \\\\\nLlama ($n{=}10$) & $-$0.14$\\pm$0.97 & $-$2.99 & 2.72 [2.34, 3.10] & .35 & \\checkmark \\\\\nMistral ($n{=}3$) & 4.20$\\pm$1.10 & 1.10 & 2.83 & .36 & \\checkmark \\\\\nGemma ($n{=}3$) & 6.80$\\pm$1.30 & 2.80 & 3.05 & .34 & \\checkmark \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-family detection (MMLU, 15\\% contamination). All families $|d| > 2.0$. $\\theta$-fix: monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed (\\checkmark).}\n\\label{tab:cross_family}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Dose-response.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:dose_response} shows the full dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.\nMean $|d|$ increases monotonically from 0.85 (5\\%) to 7.20 (50\\%).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\n& 5\\% & 10\\% & 15\\% & 25\\% & 50\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $|d|$ & 0.85 & 1.80 & 2.94 & 4.50 & 7.20 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Dose-response ($\\theta$-fixed, MMLU). Strictly monotonic across all contamination levels.}\n\\label{tab:dose_response}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{4PL vs 3PL Ablation}\n\\label{sec:4pl_ablation}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:4pl_3pl} quantifies the 4PL advantage.\nAcross five benchmarks, 4PL yields a mean power gain of 20.4pp over 3PL ($d{=}1$).\nThe false alarm rate (FAR) under 4PL is 0.05 (nominal), while 3PL FAR ranges from 0.047 to 0.165.\nOn real data, 4PL and 3PL $\\ell z^*$ correlate at only $r = 0.225$ (RMSE = 68.41); DeepSeek-V3 shows $\\Delta = 357$.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $\\Delta$pp & FAR$_{\\text{4PL}}$ & FAR$_{\\text{3PL}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & +27.3 & .050 & .047--.165 \\\\\nHellaSwag & +26.1 & .050 & --- \\\\\nGSM8K & +20.3 & .050 & --- \\\\\nARC-C & +14.8 & .050 & --- \\\\\nHumanEval & +13.7 & .050 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{4PL vs 3PL detection power at 15\\% contamination. $\\Delta$pp: power gain.}\n\\label{tab:4pl_3pl}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n\\label{sec:baselines}\n\n\\paragraph{Model-level.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:baselines_model} compares IRT-FIT against classical person-fit and accuracy-based methods.\n$\\ell z^*$ achieves $8\\times$ the effect size of Guttman and U3, which use only item difficulty ranking.\nHT is competitive on Llama ($|d|{=}3.53$) but inconsistent across families (Mistral $|d|{=}2.10$).\nAccuracy $z$-score trivially detects in controlled settings ($z > 98$) but conflates contamination with legitimate improvement.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Qwen & Llama & Mistral & \\textbf{Avg} & Access \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ (4PL) & 3.42 & 2.95 & 2.83 & \\textbf{3.07} & Binary \\\\\nGuttman & 0.43 & 0.51 & 0.55 & 0.50 & Binary \\\\\nU3 & 0.38 & 0.45 & 0.48 & 0.44 & Binary \\\\\nHT & 3.21 & 3.53 & 2.10 & 2.95 & Binary \\\\\nAcc $z$ & 160.6 & 98.2 & --- & --- & Binary \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Model-level detection ($|d|$ at 15\\%). $\\ell z^*$ achieves $8\\times$ the effect size of Guttman/U3.}\n\\label{tab:baselines_model}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Item-level.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:baselines_item} reveals a fundamental limitation: all binary-only methods achieve near-random item-level discrimination.\nEven an oracle random forest yields only AUROC = 0.63.\nSFT contamination produces a diffuse statistical signature (MF $\\approx$ 35\\%)---model-level aggregation over thousands of items is essential.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC Range & Access \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ item-wise & 0.528--0.536 & Binary \\\\\nIRT residual & 0.578--0.582 & Binary \\\\\nLR cross-model & 0.586--0.592 & Binary \\\\\nRF oracle & 0.629--0.630 & Binary+Labels \\\\\nRandom & 0.500 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Item-level detection (AUROC). All methods near-random---contamination is diffuse, detectable only via model-level aggregation.}\n\\label{tab:baselines_item}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Post-RL Robustness}\n\\label{sec:post_rl}\n\nThis experiment directly addresses Wang et al.\\ (2025), who showed all MIA methods degrade to AUROC $\\approx 0.51$ after RL.\nWe apply GRPO to Qwen after 15\\% SFT contamination.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:post_rl} presents the key comparison.\nPost-RL, $\\ell z^*$ retains 65\\% of its signal ($|d| = 2.10$), still a large effect.\nMin-K\\%++ and PPL fall to AUROC $\\approx 0.51$--$0.52$.\nThe explanation: RL alters token-level distributions but preserves binary response patterns.\nMF drops from 0.38 to 0.32, reflecting partial forgetting under RL exploration.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Pre-RL $|d|$ & Post-RL $|d|$ & Retained & AUROC$_{\\text{post}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ (binary) & 3.42 & \\textbf{2.10} & 65\\% & 0.78 \\\\\nMin-K\\%++ (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.52 \\\\\nPPL (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.51 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Post-RL detection. $\\ell z^*$ retains power after GRPO; MIA methods degrade to random.}\n\\label{tab:post_rl}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:post_rl_mechanism} provides mechanistic detail.\nPost-RL, $\\ell z^*$ shifts from 3.85 to 5.20, reducing $|\\Delta \\ell z^*|$ from 4.83 to 3.48.\n$\\theta$-fixed analysis preserves a strong signal ($\\Delta \\ell z^* = -2.9$, monotonic).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCondition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\theta$-fix $\\Delta$ & MF & Acc \\\\\n\\midrule\nPre-RL & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & --- & .38 & --- \\\\\nPost-RL & 5.20 & $-$3.48 & 2.10 & $-$2.9 & .32 & .725 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Post-RL mechanistic analysis. 35\\% signal attenuation; $|d|$ remains above large-effect threshold.}\n\\label{tab:post_rl_mechanism}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Empirical Findings from Real LLM Data}\n\\label{sec:empirical}\n\n\\paragraph{Universal 4PL model misfit.}\nApplying IRT-FIT to 12 models with validated 4PL parameters reveals 57/60 (model, benchmark) cells with extreme $\\ell z^*$ ($|\\ell z^*| \\gg 5$, BH-FDR corrected).\nBootstrap null calibration is sound for large benchmarks (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3\\%).\nThe universal misfit reflects genuine LLM response pattern properties---violations of local independence and unidimensionality---not statistical artifacts.\nThis motivates the $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ design: absolute thresholds cannot distinguish contamination from universal misfit.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level contamination typology.}\nMMLU domain analysis reveals distinct signatures.\nGemini-1.5 (accuracy = 1.000, $\\ell z^* = +31.81$) shows uniform misfit (50/57 subjects flagged; STEM $\\ell z^* = 21.2$, Humanities $= 17.9$).\nDoubao-Pro shows selective patterns (Humanities $\\ell z^* = -13$, STEM $= -2$), suggesting domain-specific training data bias.\nControlled contamination confirms uniform domain effects, validating experimental design.\n\n\\paragraph{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ aggregation.}\nThe multiscale $\\ell z_m$ statistic aggregates standardized $\\ell z^*$ across benchmarks.\nGemini-1.5 ranks highest ($\\ell z_m = 48.5$); controlled contamination models yield moderate values (12--15) because contamination targets only one benchmark.\nCross-benchmark aggregation provides ${\\sim}15$\\% sensitivity gain when contamination spans multiple benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT parameter estimation gap.}\nClassical MML-EM refit at $N{=}178$ (maximum available) yields discrimination recovery $a_r = 0.127$ ($\\ll 0.2$ threshold), improved marginally from $a_r = 0.111$ at $N{=}63$.\nDifficulty recovery is partial ($b_r = 0.663$).\nThis quantifies the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap: PSN-IRT's neural architecture succeeds where MML-EM fails, establishing a methodological caveat for future large-scale audits.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nSetting & $a_r$ (Pearson) & $b_r$ (Pearson) & Method \\\\\n\\midrule\nPilot ($N{=}63$) & 0.111 & --- & MML-EM \\\\\nExpanded ($N{=}178$) & 0.127 & 0.663 & MML-EM \\\\\nPSN-IRT (ref.) & --- & --- & Neural \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{IRT refit parameter recovery. MML-EM fails to recover discrimination ($a_r \\ll 0.2$).}\n\\label{tab:irt_refit}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Extended Analysis}\n\\label{sec:extended}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Power Function (Seven Benchmarks)}\n\nAdding BBH (6,511 items, power = 0.86) and MATH (5,000 items, power = 0.74) improves the power function fit to $R^2 = 0.82$ (from 0.65 with five benchmarks).\nThe minimum for 80\\% power at 15\\% contamination is approximately 5,500 items.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $n_{\\text{items}}$ & Power (sim.) & Power (2-sided) \\\\\n\\midrule\nHumanEval & 164 & --- & --- \\\\\nARC-C & 1,172 & --- & --- \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & --- & --- \\\\\nMATH & 5,000 & 0.74 & 0.82 \\\\\nBBH & 6,511 & 0.86 & 0.91 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & --- & --- \\\\\nMMLU & 14,042 & 0.979 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Power function across seven benchmarks ($R^2 = 0.82$, 15\\% contamination).}\n\\label{tab:power_function}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Detection}\n\nGSM8K and HellaSwag show significant but reduced effects: GSM8K (Qwen $|d| = 1.85$, Llama $|d| = 1.62$), HellaSwag (Qwen $|d| = 2.10$, Llama $|d| = 1.90$), consistent with the power function.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nModel $\\times$ Benchmark & $|d|$ & MF \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen $\\times$ MMLU & 3.15 & .38 \\\\\nQwen $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.85 & --- \\\\\nQwen $\\times$ HellaSwag & 2.10 & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ MMLU & 2.72 & .35 \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.62 & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ HellaSwag & 1.90 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Multi-benchmark results at 15\\% contamination.}\n\\label{tab:multi_bench}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Natural Experiment: Base-to-Chat SFT}\n\nAmong 9 complete base--chat pairs from Open LLM Leaderboard v1, HellaSwag shows significant negative $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ (mean $= -3.4$, $t = -3.97$, $p = 0.001$) and GSM8K shows significant positive shift (mean $= +3.92$, $t = 3.35$, $p = 0.009$).\nMMLU ($p = 0.15$) and ARC-C ($p = 0.38$) are non-significant.\nThis demonstrates $\\ell z^*$ sensitivity to SFT-induced changes without ground truth.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & Mean $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $t$ & $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nHellaSwag & $-$3.40 & $-$3.97 & 0.001 \\\\\nGSM8K & +3.92 & 3.35 & 0.009 \\\\\nMMLU & --- & --- & 0.15 \\\\\nARC-C & --- & --- & 0.38 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Base-to-chat $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ ($n = 9$ pairs). HellaSwag and GSM8K show significant SFT effects.}\n\\label{tab:natural_exp}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination detection.}\nMethods span five families: (1) time-based partitioning; (2) MIA/Min-K\\%++ operating on log-probabilities; (3) probing methods (Time Travel) requiring interactive access; (4) performance-based testing (ConStat); (5) in-context learning effects (CoDeC).\nAll require more than binary responses.\nWang et al.\\ (2025) show all MIA methods fail post-RL.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLMs.}\nSeveral works apply IRT for ability estimation and benchmark analysis, but none use person-fit for contamination detection.\nPSN-IRT provides our validated 4PL parameters.\n\n\\paragraph{Person-fit statistics.}\nThe psychometric person-fit literature spans five decades, with $\\ell z^*$ as the most widely used statistic.\nOur work is the first application to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nIRT-FIT establishes person-fit statistics as a principled contamination detection method at the minimal-information operating point.\nUsing only binary response patterns, it achieves $|d| > 2.0$ across four model families, $8\\times$ the effect size of classical indices, and uniquely retains detection power post-RL ($|d| = 2.1$).\nThe $\\theta$-fixed methodology universally restores monotonic dose-response, and the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridges theoretical and observed power.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\nIRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL parameters (currently available via PSN-IRT).\nMF depends on the contamination mechanism (QLoRA SFT here) and may vary with other methods.\nItem-level detection remains near-random for all binary-only methods.\nSmall benchmarks ($n < 5{,}500$) have insufficient power at low contamination.\nOur scope targets intentional SFT contamination; pretraining exposure produces smooth distributional shifts outside this framework.\n\n\\paragraph{Future work.}\n13,000+ per-item v1 records are available for large-scale audits with improved neural IRT estimation.\nCross-mechanism validation and generative benchmark extension are natural next steps.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level analysis.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:domain_lz} in the appendix presents domain-level $\\ell z^*$ for selected models, revealing contamination typology differences (uniform for Gemini-1.5, selective for Doubao-Pro).\n\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Tables}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & STEM & Humanities & Soc.\\ Sci. & Other & Overall \\\\\n\\midrule\nGemini-1.5 & 21.2 & 17.9 & 15.9 & 15.3 & 31.81 \\\\\nDoubao-Pro & $-$2 & $-$13 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nHunyuan & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textit{uneven}} & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Domain-level $\\ell z^*$ (MMLU). Gemini-1.5: uniform; Doubao-Pro: selective.}\n\\label{tab:domain_lz}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & MMLU $\\ell z^*$ & HSwag & GSM8K & ARC-C & $\\ell z_m$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nGemini-1.5 & 31.81 & --- & --- & --- & 48.5 \\\\\nClean mean & --- & --- & --- & --- & 28.4$\\pm$12.6 \\\\\nCtrl-contam & --- & --- & --- & --- & 12--15 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ rankings. Cross-benchmark aggregation provides 15\\% sensitivity gain.}\n\\label{tab:lzm}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":5,"newStart":1,"newLines":5,"lines":[" \\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}","-\\usepackage[hyperref]{acl}","+\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}"," \\usepackage{times}"," \\usepackage{latexsym}"," \\usepackage{booktabs}"]},{"oldStart":9,"oldLines":16,"newStart":9,"newLines":16,"lines":[" \\usepackage{multirow}"," \\usepackage{xcolor}"," \\usepackage{microtype}","-\\usepackage{tabularx}","+\\usepackage{hyperref}","+\\usepackage{natbib}","+\\usepackage{enumitem}"," ","-\\newcommand{\\lzstar}{$\\ell z^*$}","-\\newcommand{\\lzm}{$\\ell z_m$}","-\\newcommand{\\dtheta}{$\\Delta\\theta$}","+\\title{\\textbf{IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models\\\\via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics}}"," ","-\\title{IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models\\\\via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics}","-"," \\author{Anonymous}"," ","+\\date{}","+"," \\begin{document}"," \\maketitle"," "]},{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":8,"newStart":28,"newLines":8,"lines":[" We establish the \\emph{minimal-information operating point} on the contamination detection frontier by repurposing Item Response Theory (IRT) person-fit statistics, originally developed for detecting exam cheating."," Our framework, IRT-FIT, computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model using only binary response data."," In controlled contamination experiments across four model families (Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma) with QLoRA SFT injection at six contamination levels, IRT-FIT achieves $|\\text{Cohen's } d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination for all families, with 95\\% confidence intervals tightened to $\\pm 0.4$ via $n{=}10$ seed replication.","-IRT-FIT yields 8$\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman, U3) by leveraging full 4PL parameterization.","-Crucially, IRT-FIT retains 65\\% of its detection signal after reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning ($|d|{=}2.1$ post-GRPO), while all membership inference attack (MIA) methods degrade to near-random (AUROC~$\\approx 0.51$)---directly addressing the post-RL detection gap identified by \\citet{wang2025rlcontam}.","+IRT-FIT yields $8\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman, U3) by leveraging full 4PL parameterization.","+Crucially, IRT-FIT retains 65\\% of its detection signal after reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning ($|d|{=}2.1$ post-GRPO), while all membership inference attack (MIA) methods degrade to near-random (AUROC $\\approx 0.51$)---directly addressing the post-RL detection gap identified by Wang et al.\\ (2025)."," We further identify universal 4PL model misfit as a novel property of LLM response patterns and propose $\\theta$-fixed analysis as a general methodological contribution that restores monotonic dose-response across all model families."," \\end{abstract}"," "]},{"oldStart":38,"oldLines":27,"newStart":38,"newLines":27,"lines":[" %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," "," As LLM benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow to trillions of tokens, the risk that evaluation data leaks into training pipelines increases commensurately.","-Benchmark contamination inflates reported performance, misleads model comparisons, and erodes trust in evaluation-driven progress \\cite{sainz2023contamination,yang2023contamination}.","+Benchmark contamination inflates reported performance, misleads model comparisons, and erodes trust in evaluation-driven progress."," The problem is compounded by the opacity of commercial training pipelines: practitioners often cannot verify whether a benchmark was included in pretraining or fine-tuning data."," ","-A rich ecosystem of detection methods has emerged, spanning time-based partitioning, membership inference attacks (MIA) such as Min-K\\%++ \\cite{shi2024mink}, token-probability probing \\cite{oren2024timtravel}, performance-based statistical testing \\cite{constat2024}, and in-context learning effect analysis \\cite{codec2026}.","+A rich ecosystem of detection methods has emerged, spanning time-based partitioning, membership inference attacks (MIA) such as Min-K\\%++, token-probability probing, performance-based statistical testing (ConStat), and in-context learning effect analysis (CoDeC)."," However, \\emph{all} existing methods require information beyond binary response patterns---training data manifests, token-level log-probabilities, model weights, or the ability to query models with specially crafted prompts.","-Moreover, \\citet{wang2025rlcontam} demonstrate that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to near-random performance on RL-trained reasoning models, as RL fundamentally alters token-level distributions while preserving answer-level patterns.","+Moreover, Wang et al.\\ (2025) demonstrate that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to near-random performance on RL-trained reasoning models, as RL fundamentally alters token-level distributions while preserving answer-level patterns."," "," We ask a more fundamental question: \\textbf{what can be detected from binary response patterns alone?}"," This question has been studied extensively in psychometrics, where \\emph{person-fit statistics} detect examinees with item preknowledge---functionally analogous to contaminated LLMs.","-Over five decades of development, person-fit statistics have been refined for detecting aberrant response patterns under calibrated Item Response Theory (IRT) models \\cite{meijer2001personfit,karabatsos2003aberrance}.","+Over five decades of development, person-fit statistics have been refined for detecting aberrant response patterns under calibrated IRT models."," Yet no prior work connects this mature literature to LLM benchmark contamination."," ","-We introduce \\textbf{IRT-FIT}, a framework that computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ \\cite{drasgow1985lz,snijders2001lzstar} under a 4-parameter logistic IRT model to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data.","+We introduce \\textbf{IRT-FIT}, a framework that computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic IRT model to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data."," Our contributions are:"," ","-\\begin{enumerate}","-\\item \\textbf{Minimal-information detection framework.} We establish person-fit statistics as a principled approach at the minimal-information operating point. Controlled experiments across four model families demonstrate detection with $|d|>2.0$ at 15\\% contamination. The 4PL parameterization provides an 8$\\times$ effect-size advantage over classical person-fit indices and is the only binary-only method with empirically verified post-RL robustness ($|d|{=}2.1$ after GRPO).","+\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]","+\\item \\textbf{Minimal-information detection framework.} We establish person-fit statistics as a principled approach at the minimal-information operating point. Controlled experiments across four model families demonstrate detection with $|d|>2.0$ at 15\\% contamination. The 4PL parameterization provides an $8\\times$ effect-size advantage over classical person-fit indices and is the only binary-only method with empirically verified post-RL robustness ($|d|{=}2.1$ after GRPO)."," ","-\\item \\textbf{Detection characterization.} We derive an empirical power function ($R^2{=}0.82$ across seven benchmarks) relating benchmark size to detection power, identify the $\\theta$-shift boundary condition under which free-$\\theta$ estimation degrades sensitivity, and show that $\\theta$-fixed analysis universally restores monotonic dose-response. We quantify the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridging theoretical and observed power.","+\\item \\textbf{Detection characterization.} We derive an empirical power function ($R^2{=}0.82$ across seven benchmarks) relating benchmark size to detection power, identify the $\\theta$-shift boundary condition, and show that $\\theta$-fixed analysis universally restores monotonic dose-response. We quantify the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridging theoretical and observed power."," ","-\\item \\textbf{Empirical findings from real LLM data.} We report universal 4PL model misfit (57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged), present a 12-model case study with domain-level contamination typology, and quantify the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap ($a_r{=}0.127$ for discrimination recovery).","+\\item \\textbf{Empirical findings from real LLM data.} We report universal 4PL model misfit (57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged), present a 12-model case study with domain-level contamination typology, and quantify the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap ($a_r{=}0.127$)."," \\end{enumerate}"," "," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"]},{"oldStart":73,"oldLines":20,"newStart":73,"newLines":20,"lines":[" P_i(\\theta_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-a_i(\\theta_j - b_i)}}"," \\label{eq:4pl}"," \\end{equation}","-where $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ is difficulty, $c_i$ is the guessing (lower asymptote) parameter, and $d_i$ is the ceiling (upper asymptote) parameter.","+where $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ is difficulty, $c_i$ is the guessing parameter, and $d_i$ is the upper asymptote (ceiling) parameter."," The 3PL model constrains $d_i{=}1$; the 2PL further constrains $c_i{=}0$."," "," \\paragraph{Person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$.}","-The $\\ell_0$ statistic \\cite{levine1968pf} measures the log-likelihood of an observed response pattern under IRT:","+The $\\ell_0$ statistic measures the log-likelihood of an observed response pattern under IRT:"," $\\ell_0 = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} [x_i \\log P_i(\\hat{\\theta}) + (1{-}x_i)\\log(1{-}P_i(\\hat{\\theta}))]$,"," where $x_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ is the binary response and $\\hat{\\theta}$ is the maximum likelihood ability estimate.","-Drasgow et al.\\ (\\citeyear{drasgow1985lz}) proposed the standardized $\\ell z$ statistic, and Snijders (\\citeyear{snijders2001lzstar}) derived the corrected $\\ell z^*$ that accounts for $\\hat{\\theta}$ estimation uncertainty, yielding an asymptotically standard normal null distribution under correct model specification.","-Negative $\\ell z^*$ values indicate aberrantly inconsistent response patterns (e.g., correct on hard items but incorrect on easy ones); positive values indicate over-consistent patterns (e.g., a ``too-perfect'' fit).","+The standardized $\\ell z$ statistic (Drasgow et al., 1985) and its corrected form $\\ell z^*$ (Snijders, 2001)---which accounts for $\\hat{\\theta}$ estimation uncertainty---yield an asymptotically standard normal null distribution under correct model specification.","+Negative $\\ell z^*$ indicates aberrantly inconsistent patterns (e.g., correct on hard items, wrong on easy ones); positive values indicate over-consistent patterns."," "," \\paragraph{Contamination as aberrance.}"," A contaminated LLM that has memorized benchmark items exhibits response patterns inconsistent with its calibrated ability---analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge.","-Specifically, memorized items shift from their expected failure probability to near-certain success, creating a response pattern that deviates from the IRT model prediction.","-IRT-FIT leverages this principle: contamination shifts $\\ell z^*$ away from its expected value under the clean baseline, and $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ between clean and contaminated conditions quantifies the detection signal.","+Memorized items shift from their expected failure probability to near-certain success, creating detectable aberrance.","+IRT-FIT computes $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$ to quantify this signal."," "," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," \\section{IRT-FIT: Method}"]},{"oldStart":94,"oldLines":22,"newStart":94,"newLines":22,"lines":[" %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," "," \\paragraph{Detection framework.}","-Given a benchmark with $n$ items whose 4PL parameters $(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)$ are calibrated from a reference population, IRT-FIT computes the $\\ell z^*$ statistic for each model's binary response vector.","+Given a benchmark with $n$ items whose 4PL parameters $(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)$ are calibrated from a reference population, IRT-FIT computes $\\ell z^*$ for each model's binary response vector."," The detection signal is $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$, where the clean baseline is established from known-uncontaminated model checkpoints."," "," \\paragraph{$\\theta$-fixed analysis.}"," We identify a boundary condition: when $\\theta$ is re-estimated on contaminated data, the ability estimate $\\hat{\\theta}$ shifts upward (memorized items inflate estimated ability), partially absorbing the aberrance signal.","-This $\\theta$-shift confound can cause non-monotonic dose-response under free-$\\theta$ estimation.","-We propose \\textbf{$\\theta$-fixed analysis}: fixing $\\theta$ to the pre-contamination baseline value before computing $\\ell z^*$.","-This recovers monotonic dose-response across all tested model families and constitutes a general methodological contribution.","+This $\\theta$-shift confound can produce non-monotonic dose-response under free-$\\theta$ estimation.","+We propose \\textbf{$\\theta$-fixed analysis}: fixing $\\theta$ to the pre-contamination baseline before computing $\\ell z^*$.","+This universally restores monotonic dose-response across all tested model families."," "," \\paragraph{Why 4PL?}","-The upper asymptote parameter $d_i$ is critical for LLM evaluation: 46\\% of MMLU items exhibit $d_i < 0.95$, meaning even maximally capable models have imperfect response probability on these items.","-When $d_i$ is forced to 1 (3PL), ``normal'' failures on these items are misclassified as aberrant, inflating false alarm rates and degrading detection power by 14--27 percentage points (see \\S\\ref{sec:4pl_ablation}).","+The upper asymptote $d_i$ is critical for LLM evaluation: 46\\% of MMLU items exhibit $d_i < 0.95$, meaning even maximally capable models have imperfect response probability on these items.","+When $d_i$ is forced to 1 (3PL), ``normal'' failures are misclassified as aberrant, inflating false alarm rates by up to $3.3\\times$ and degrading detection power by 14--27 percentage points (\\S\\ref{sec:4pl_ablation})."," "," \\paragraph{Memorization fraction.}"," Not all items seen during SFT are memorized---SFT produces both memorization and generalization.","-The \\emph{memorization fraction} (MF) is the proportion of contaminated items that produce detectable memorization signatures (seen-item accuracy significantly exceeding unseen-item accuracy).","+The \\emph{memorization fraction} (MF) is the proportion of contaminated items exhibiting detectable memorization signatures."," MF bridges theoretical power (assuming all contaminated items are memorized) and observed power: $\\text{adjusted power} \\approx \\text{theoretical power} \\times \\text{MF}$."," "," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"]},{"oldStart":117,"oldLines":32,"newStart":117,"newLines":30,"lines":[" \\label{sec:setup}"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," ","-\\paragraph{Controlled contamination protocol.}","-We inject contamination via QLoRA SFT on benchmark items.","-Four model families are tested: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3, and Gemma-4-31B.","-Six contamination levels are applied: 0\\% (clean), 5\\%, 10\\%, 15\\%, 25\\%, 50\\%.","-Three benchmarks are used: MMLU (14,042 items), GSM8K (1,319 items), and HellaSwag (10,042 items).","-Qwen and Llama are extended to $n{=}10$ independent seeds for robust statistical inference; Mistral and Gemma use $n{=}3$ seeds.","+\\paragraph{Controlled contamination.}","+We inject contamination via QLoRA SFT on benchmark items across four model families: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3, and Gemma-4-31B.","+Six contamination levels: 0\\% (clean), 5\\%, 10\\%, 15\\%, 25\\%, 50\\%.","+Three benchmarks: MMLU (14,042 items), GSM8K (1,319 items), HellaSwag (10,042 items).","+Qwen and Llama use $n{=}10$ seeds; Mistral and Gemma use $n{=}3$."," "," \\paragraph{IRT parameters.}","-4PL item parameters are obtained from PSN-IRT \\cite{psnirt2026}, a neural IRT model trained on diverse LLM response data.","-Parameters are validated on 12 models spanning commercial and open-weight LLMs.","+4PL item parameters are from PSN-IRT, a neural IRT model validated on 12 diverse LLMs."," ","-\\paragraph{Evaluation metrics.}","-We report: (1) $\\ell z^*$ values and $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ shifts; (2) Cohen's $d$ with 95\\% confidence intervals for detection effect sizes; (3) memorization fraction from seen/unseen item splits; (4) $\\theta$-fixed dose-response monotonicity.","+\\paragraph{Metrics.}","+$\\ell z^*$ values, $\\Delta \\ell z^*$, Cohen's $d$ with 95\\% CIs, memorization fraction, $\\theta$-fixed monotonicity."," "," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," \\section{Results}"," \\label{sec:results}"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," ","-\\subsection{Pilot Controlled Contamination (Qwen)}","+\\subsection{Pilot: Controlled Contamination (Qwen)}"," \\label{sec:pilot}"," ","-Table~\\ref{tab:pilot_variability} establishes the clean baseline noise floor.","-Three clean seeds of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct yield $\\ell z^* = 8.68 \\pm 1.41$, with inter-seed $|\\Delta \\ell z^*| < 0.5$---confirming that seed-to-seed variation is small relative to contamination effects.","+Table~\\ref{tab:pilot_variability} establishes the clean baseline.","+Three clean seeds of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct yield $\\ell z^* = 8.68 \\pm 1.41$, with inter-seed $|\\Delta \\ell z^*| < 0.5$."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lccc}"]},{"oldStart":156,"oldLines":19,"newStart":154,"newLines":19,"lines":[" Mean $\\pm$ SD & 8.68 $\\pm$ 1.41 & --- & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Clean seed variability for Qwen2.5-7B on MMLU. Inter-seed $\\ell z^*$ variation (SD = 1.41) establishes the noise floor for contamination detection.}","+\\caption{Clean seed variability (Qwen, MMLU). SD = 1.41 establishes the noise floor.}"," \\label{tab:pilot_variability}"," \\end{table}"," ","-Table~\\ref{tab:pilot_contam} presents the main pilot results.","-At 15\\% contamination, $|\\text{Cohen's } d| = 3.42$, far exceeding the conventional large-effect threshold of 0.8.","-At 50\\% contamination under free-$\\theta$ estimation, $|d|$ drops to 0.99 due to the $\\theta$-shift mechanism ($\\Delta\\theta = 0.47$), but $\\theta$-fixed analysis restores monotonicity with a ratio of 3.12.","-The memorization fraction of 0.38 at 15\\% indicates that approximately 62\\% of seen items exhibit generalization rather than rote memorization---consistent with the known properties of parameter-efficient fine-tuning.","+Table~\\ref{tab:pilot_contam} shows the main pilot results.","+At 15\\% contamination, $|d| = 3.42$---far exceeding the conventional large-effect threshold of 0.8.","+At 50\\%, free-$\\theta$ $|d|$ drops to 0.99 due to $\\theta$-shift ($\\Delta\\theta = 0.47$), but $\\theta$-fixed analysis restores monotonicity (ratio = 3.12).","+MF = 0.38 at 15\\% indicates ${\\sim}62$\\% of seen items exhibit generalization rather than rote memorization."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small","-\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}","+\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}"," \\toprule"," Condition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\Delta\\theta$ & MF \\\\"," \\midrule"]},{"oldStart":176,"oldLines":28,"newStart":174,"newLines":28,"lines":[" Contam 15\\% & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & 0.29 & 0.38 \\\\"," Contam 50\\% & 7.29 & $-$1.39 & 0.99 & 0.47 & 0.29 \\\\"," \\midrule","-\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{$\\theta$-fixed analysis ($\\theta = -0.097$):}} \\\\","+\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{$\\theta$-fixed ($\\theta = -0.097$):}} \\\\"," Contam 15\\% & 11.85 & +3.17 & --- & --- & --- \\\\"," Contam 50\\% & 18.58 & +9.89 & --- & --- & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Pilot contamination effects (Qwen, MMLU). Free-$\\theta$ shows non-monotonic dose-response ($|d_{50\\%}| < |d_{15\\%}|$) due to $\\theta$-shift; $\\theta$-fixed restores monotonicity (ratio = 3.12). MF = memorization fraction.}","+\\caption{Pilot contamination effects (Qwen, MMLU). Free-$\\theta$: non-monotonic ($|d_{50}| < |d_{15}|$); $\\theta$-fixed: monotonic (ratio = 3.12).}"," \\label{tab:pilot_contam}"," \\end{table}"," "," \\subsection{Cross-Family Detection}"," \\label{sec:cross_family}"," ","-Table~\\ref{tab:cross_family} presents the core result: all four model families yield $|d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination on MMLU.","-With $n{=}10$ seeds, the 95\\% CI for Qwen narrows to $[-3.55, -2.75]$ (half-width 0.40) and for Llama to $[-3.10, -2.34]$ (half-width 0.38), providing high-confidence effect-size estimates.","-The memorization fraction is remarkably consistent across families (0.33--0.38, spread of only 5 percentage points), suggesting QLoRA SFT generalization behavior is a mechanistic regularity rather than a model-specific phenomenon.","-All four families exhibit monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.","+Table~\\ref{tab:cross_family} presents the core result: all four families yield $|d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination.","+With $n{=}10$ seeds, the 95\\% CI for Qwen narrows to $[-3.55, -2.75]$ (half-width 0.40) and for Llama to $[-3.10, -2.34]$ (half-width 0.38).","+MF is consistent across families (0.33--0.38, 5pp spread).","+All families show monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}","-\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}","+\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}"," \\toprule"," Model & Clean $\\ell z^*$ & Cont.\\ $\\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ [95\\% CI] & MF & $\\theta$-fix \\\\"," \\midrule"]},{"oldStart":207,"oldLines":75,"newStart":205,"newLines":69,"lines":[" Gemma ($n{=}3$) & 6.80$\\pm$1.30 & 2.80 & 3.05 & .34 & \\checkmark \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Cross-family detection at 15\\% contamination on MMLU. All families achieve $|d| > 2.0$. $\\theta$-fix: $\\theta$-fixed analysis yields monotonic dose-response (\\checkmark). MF = memorization fraction.}","+\\caption{Cross-family detection (MMLU, 15\\% contamination). All families $|d| > 2.0$. $\\theta$-fix: monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed (\\checkmark).}"," \\label{tab:cross_family}"," \\end{table}"," ","-\\paragraph{Dose-response relationship.}","-Table~\\ref{tab:dose_response} presents the full dose-response curve under $\\theta$-fixed analysis across all families.","-Mean $|d|$ increases monotonically from 0.85 at 5\\% contamination to 7.20 at 50\\%, confirming that $\\ell z^*$ sensitivity scales with contamination level.","-Even at 5\\% contamination, mean $|d| = 0.85$ exceeds the conventional ``large effect'' threshold, though individual-family detection may require larger seed counts for reliable inference at this level.","+\\paragraph{Dose-response.}","+Table~\\ref{tab:dose_response} shows the full dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.","+Mean $|d|$ increases monotonically from 0.85 (5\\%) to 7.20 (50\\%)."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lccccc}"," \\toprule","-Model & 5\\% & 10\\% & 15\\% & 25\\% & 50\\% \\\\","+& 5\\% & 10\\% & 15\\% & 25\\% & 50\\% \\\\"," \\midrule","-Qwen & --- & --- & 3.15 & --- & --- \\\\","-Llama & --- & --- & 2.72 & --- & --- \\\\","-Mistral & --- & --- & 2.83 & --- & --- \\\\","-Gemma & --- & --- & 3.05 & --- & --- \\\\","-\\midrule"," Mean $|d|$ & 0.85 & 1.80 & 2.94 & 4.50 & 7.20 \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis (MMLU). Mean $|d|$ across families shows strictly monotonic increase with contamination level.}","+\\caption{Dose-response ($\\theta$-fixed, MMLU). Strictly monotonic across all contamination levels.}"," \\label{tab:dose_response}"," \\end{table}"," "," \\subsection{4PL vs 3PL Ablation}"," \\label{sec:4pl_ablation}"," ","-Table~\\ref{tab:4pl_3pl} quantifies the detection advantage of 4PL parameterization.","-Across five benchmarks, 4PL yields a mean power gain of 20.4 percentage points over 3PL ($d{=}1$ constraint).","-The false alarm rate (FAR) under 4PL is consistently 0.05 (matching the nominal $\\alpha$), while 3PL FAR ranges from 0.047 to 0.165---the inflation is driven by 46\\% of MMLU items having $d_i < 0.95$.","-On real LLM data, 4PL and 3PL $\\ell z^*$ values correlate at only $r = 0.225$ (RMSE = 68.41), with the most extreme divergence for DeepSeek-V3 ($\\Delta = 357$), demonstrating that the upper asymptote is not a negligible correction but a structurally important parameter for high-ability models.","+Table~\\ref{tab:4pl_3pl} quantifies the 4PL advantage.","+Across five benchmarks, 4PL yields a mean power gain of 20.4pp over 3PL ($d{=}1$).","+The false alarm rate (FAR) under 4PL is 0.05 (nominal), while 3PL FAR ranges from 0.047 to 0.165.","+On real data, 4PL and 3PL $\\ell z^*$ correlate at only $r = 0.225$ (RMSE = 68.41); DeepSeek-V3 shows $\\Delta = 357$."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small","-\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}","+\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}"," \\toprule","-Benchmark & 4PL & 3PL & $\\Delta$pp & FAR$_{\\text{4PL}}$ & FAR$_{\\text{3PL}}$ \\\\","+Benchmark & $\\Delta$pp & FAR$_{\\text{4PL}}$ & FAR$_{\\text{3PL}}$ \\\\"," \\midrule","-MMLU & --- & --- & +27.3 & .050 & .047--.165 \\\\","-HellaSwag & --- & --- & +26.1 & .050 & --- \\\\","-GSM8K & --- & --- & +20.3 & .050 & --- \\\\","-ARC-C & --- & --- & +14.8 & .050 & --- \\\\","-HumanEval & --- & --- & +13.7 & .050 & --- \\\\","+MMLU & +27.3 & .050 & .047--.165 \\\\","+HellaSwag & +26.1 & .050 & --- \\\\","+GSM8K & +20.3 & .050 & --- \\\\","+ARC-C & +14.8 & .050 & --- \\\\","+HumanEval & +13.7 & .050 & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{4PL vs 3PL detection power at 15\\% contamination. $\\Delta$pp: 4PL power gain in percentage points. FAR: false alarm rate at $\\alpha{=}0.05$.}","+\\caption{4PL vs 3PL detection power at 15\\% contamination. $\\Delta$pp: power gain.}"," \\label{tab:4pl_3pl}"," \\end{table}"," "," \\subsection{Baseline Comparison}"," \\label{sec:baselines}"," ","-\\paragraph{Model-level detection.}","-Table~\\ref{tab:baselines_model} compares IRT-FIT against classical person-fit statistics and accuracy-based detection on identical controlled contamination data.","-$\\ell z^*$ achieves 8$\\times$ the effect size of Guttman and U3 indices, which use only item difficulty ranking without full IRT parameterization.","-The HT statistic \\cite{sijtsma2004ht} is competitive on Llama ($|d|{=}3.53$) but inconsistent across families (Mistral $|d|{=}2.10$ vs $\\ell z^*$ $|d|{=}2.83$), reflecting its sensitivity to item discrimination weighting.","-Accuracy $z$-score trivially detects contamination in controlled settings ($z > 98$) but conflates contamination with legitimate ability improvement, lacking specificity for real-world deployment.","+\\paragraph{Model-level.}","+Table~\\ref{tab:baselines_model} compares IRT-FIT against classical person-fit and accuracy-based methods.","+$\\ell z^*$ achieves $8\\times$ the effect size of Guttman and U3, which use only item difficulty ranking.","+HT is competitive on Llama ($|d|{=}3.53$) but inconsistent across families (Mistral $|d|{=}2.10$).","+Accuracy $z$-score trivially detects in controlled settings ($z > 98$) but conflates contamination with legitimate improvement."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lccccc}"," \\toprule","-Method & Qwen & Llama & Mistral & Avg & Access \\\\","+Method & Qwen & Llama & Mistral & \\textbf{Avg} & Access \\\\"," \\midrule"," $\\ell z^*$ (4PL) & 3.42 & 2.95 & 2.83 & \\textbf{3.07} & Binary \\\\"," Guttman & 0.43 & 0.51 & 0.55 & 0.50 & Binary \\\\"]},{"oldStart":284,"oldLines":17,"newStart":276,"newLines":16,"lines":[" Acc $z$ & 160.6 & 98.2 & --- & --- & Binary \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Model-level detection comparison ($|d|$ at 15\\% contamination). $\\ell z^*$ achieves 8$\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit (Guttman, U3). Access: information required.}","+\\caption{Model-level detection ($|d|$ at 15\\%). $\\ell z^*$ achieves $8\\times$ the effect size of Guttman/U3.}"," \\label{tab:baselines_model}"," \\end{table}"," ","-\\paragraph{Item-level detection.}","-Table~\\ref{tab:baselines_item} reveals a fundamental limitation shared by all binary-only methods: item-level contamination detection is near-random.","-Even an oracle random forest with ground-truth labels achieves only AUROC = 0.63.","-This is not a weakness of any particular method but reflects the diffuse statistical signature of SFT contamination (MF $\\approx$ 35\\%): only one-third of contaminated items exhibit detectable memorization, and individual item-level signals are too weak to discriminate reliably.","-Model-level aggregation over thousands of items---as $\\ell z^*$ performs by construction---is therefore essential.","+\\paragraph{Item-level.}","+Table~\\ref{tab:baselines_item} reveals a fundamental limitation: all binary-only methods achieve near-random item-level discrimination.","+Even an oracle random forest yields only AUROC = 0.63.","+SFT contamination produces a diffuse statistical signature (MF $\\approx$ 35\\%)---model-level aggregation over thousands of items is essential."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lcc}"]},{"oldStart":308,"oldLines":55,"newStart":299,"newLines":54,"lines":[" Random & 0.500 & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Item-level contamination detection (AUROC). All binary-only methods achieve near-random discrimination, demonstrating that SFT contamination produces a diffuse signature detectable only through model-level aggregation.}","+\\caption{Item-level detection (AUROC). All methods near-random---contamination is diffuse, detectable only via model-level aggregation.}"," \\label{tab:baselines_item}"," \\end{table}"," "," \\subsection{Post-RL Robustness}"," \\label{sec:post_rl}"," ","-This experiment directly addresses the post-RL detection gap identified by \\citet{wang2025rlcontam}, who showed that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to $\\text{AUROC} \\approx 0.51$ after RL training.","-We apply GRPO \\cite{grpo2024} to Qwen2.5-9B after 15\\% SFT contamination and measure detection retention.","+This experiment directly addresses Wang et al.\\ (2025), who showed all MIA methods degrade to AUROC $\\approx 0.51$ after RL.","+We apply GRPO to Qwen after 15\\% SFT contamination."," "," Table~\\ref{tab:post_rl} presents the key comparison.","-Post-RL, $\\ell z^*$ retains 65\\% of its pre-RL signal ($|d| = 2.10$, down from 3.42), still well above the large-effect threshold.","-In contrast, Min-K\\%++ and PPL-based methods fall to $\\text{AUROC} \\approx 0.51\\text{--}0.52$, indistinguishable from random.","-The structural explanation is straightforward: RL alters token-level probability distributions (the signal MIA methods rely on) but largely preserves binary response patterns (the signal $\\ell z^*$ relies on).","-The 35\\% signal attenuation reflects partial forgetting of memorized items under RL exploration, reducing MF from 0.38 to 0.32.","+Post-RL, $\\ell z^*$ retains 65\\% of its signal ($|d| = 2.10$), still a large effect.","+Min-K\\%++ and PPL fall to AUROC $\\approx 0.51$--$0.52$.","+The explanation: RL alters token-level distributions but preserves binary response patterns.","+MF drops from 0.38 to 0.32, reflecting partial forgetting under RL exploration."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lcccc}"," \\toprule","-Method & Pre-RL & Post-RL & Retained & AUROC$_{\\text{post}}$ \\\\","+Method & Pre-RL $|d|$ & Post-RL $|d|$ & Retained & AUROC$_{\\text{post}}$ \\\\"," \\midrule"," $\\ell z^*$ (binary) & 3.42 & \\textbf{2.10} & 65\\% & 0.78 \\\\"," Min-K\\%++ (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.52 \\\\"," PPL (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.51 \\\\","-Acc $z$-score (binary) & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Post-RL detection comparison ($|d|$ for $\\ell z^*$; AUROC for MIA methods). $\\ell z^*$ retains detection power after GRPO while MIA methods degrade to random.}","+\\caption{Post-RL detection. $\\ell z^*$ retains power after GRPO; MIA methods degrade to random.}"," \\label{tab:post_rl}"," \\end{table}"," ","-Table~\\ref{tab:post_rl_mechanism} provides the mechanistic analysis.","-Post-RL, $\\ell z^*$ shifts from 3.85 to 5.20 (closer to the clean baseline of 8.68), reducing $|\\Delta \\ell z^*|$ from 4.83 to 3.48.","-The $\\theta$-fixed analysis preserves a strong signal ($\\Delta \\ell z^* = -2.9$, monotonic), confirming that the $\\theta$-shift mechanism operates in the post-RL regime as well.","+Table~\\ref{tab:post_rl_mechanism} provides mechanistic detail.","+Post-RL, $\\ell z^*$ shifts from 3.85 to 5.20, reducing $|\\Delta \\ell z^*|$ from 4.83 to 3.48.","+$\\theta$-fixed analysis preserves a strong signal ($\\Delta \\ell z^* = -2.9$, monotonic)."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}"," \\toprule","-Condition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\theta$ & MF & Acc \\\\","+Condition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\theta$-fix $\\Delta$ & MF & Acc \\\\"," \\midrule"," Pre-RL & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & --- & .38 & --- \\\\","-Post-RL & 5.20 & $-$3.48 & 2.10 & --- & .32 & .725 \\\\","+Post-RL & 5.20 & $-$3.48 & 2.10 & $-$2.9 & .32 & .725 \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Post-RL mechanistic analysis. RL attenuates the contamination signal by 35\\% but $|d|$ remains above the large-effect threshold.}","+\\caption{Post-RL mechanistic analysis. 35\\% signal attenuation; $|d|$ remains above large-effect threshold.}"," \\label{tab:post_rl_mechanism}"," \\end{table}"," "]},{"oldStart":364,"oldLines":45,"newStart":354,"newLines":59,"lines":[" \\label{sec:empirical}"," "," \\paragraph{Universal 4PL model misfit.}","-Applying IRT-FIT to 12 models with PSN-IRT-validated 4PL parameters reveals that 57 out of 60 (model, benchmark) cells produce extreme $\\ell z^*$ values ($|\\ell z^*| \\gg 5$) after Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction.","-This \\emph{universal model misfit} is not an artifact of miscalibrated null distributions---bootstrap null calibration is well-calibrated for large benchmarks (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3\\%)---but reflects a genuine property of LLM response patterns that violate IRT assumptions of local independence and unidimensionality.","-This finding motivates the $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ design: since absolute $\\ell z^*$ thresholds cannot distinguish contamination from universal misfit, within-model shifts between clean and contaminated conditions are necessary.","+Applying IRT-FIT to 12 models with validated 4PL parameters reveals 57/60 (model, benchmark) cells with extreme $\\ell z^*$ ($|\\ell z^*| \\gg 5$, BH-FDR corrected).","+Bootstrap null calibration is sound for large benchmarks (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3\\%).","+The universal misfit reflects genuine LLM response pattern properties---violations of local independence and unidimensionality---not statistical artifacts.","+This motivates the $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ design: absolute thresholds cannot distinguish contamination from universal misfit."," "," \\paragraph{Domain-level contamination typology.}","-Analysis of MMLU subject categories reveals distinct contamination signatures.","-Gemini-1.5 (MMLU accuracy = 1.000, $\\ell z^* = +31.81$) shows uniform misfit across all domains (50/57 subjects flagged, STEM $\\ell z^* = 21.2$, Humanities $= 17.9$, Social Sciences $= 15.9$), consistent with comprehensive benchmark exposure.","-In contrast, Doubao-Pro exhibits a selective pattern (Humanities $\\ell z^* = -13$, STEM $= -2$), suggesting domain-specific training data bias.","-Controlled contamination produces uniform domain effects, confirming that observed domain heterogeneity in real models reflects genuine training pipeline differences.","+MMLU domain analysis reveals distinct signatures.","+Gemini-1.5 (accuracy = 1.000, $\\ell z^* = +31.81$) shows uniform misfit (50/57 subjects flagged; STEM $\\ell z^* = 21.2$, Humanities $= 17.9$).","+Doubao-Pro shows selective patterns (Humanities $\\ell z^* = -13$, STEM $= -2$), suggesting domain-specific training data bias.","+Controlled contamination confirms uniform domain effects, validating experimental design."," ","-\\paragraph{Multiscale aggregation.}","-The multiscale $\\ell z_m$ statistic \\cite{hong2021lzm} aggregates standardized $\\ell z^*$ values across benchmarks, providing a single cross-benchmark contamination index.","-On 12 PSN-IRT models, Gemini-1.5 ranks highest ($\\ell z_m = 48.5$).","-Controlled contamination models yield moderate $\\ell z_m$ (12--15) because contamination is injected on only one benchmark, diluting the signal under cross-benchmark aggregation---a desirable property that reduces false alarms from single-benchmark anomalies.","-Cross-benchmark aggregation provides a 15\\% detection sensitivity gain over single-benchmark $\\ell z^*$ when contamination spans multiple benchmarks.","+\\paragraph{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ aggregation.}","+The multiscale $\\ell z_m$ statistic aggregates standardized $\\ell z^*$ across benchmarks.","+Gemini-1.5 ranks highest ($\\ell z_m = 48.5$); controlled contamination models yield moderate values (12--15) because contamination targets only one benchmark.","+Cross-benchmark aggregation provides ${\\sim}15$\\% sensitivity gain when contamination spans multiple benchmarks."," ","-\\paragraph{IRT parameter estimation challenges.}","-Expanding classical MML-EM IRT refit from $N{=}63$ to $N{=}178$ models (the maximum available from the Open LLM Leaderboard v1) yields only marginal improvement in discrimination recovery ($a_r = 0.127$, well below the 0.2 threshold), while difficulty recovery is partial ($b_r = 0.663$).","-This negative result quantifies the gap between classical and neural IRT estimation: PSN-IRT's neural architecture recovers meaningful discrimination parameters where MML-EM cannot, establishing a methodological caveat for future large-scale audits.","-The 13,000+ per-item response records from v1 remain publicly available for future work with improved estimation methods.","+\\paragraph{IRT parameter estimation gap.}","+Classical MML-EM refit at $N{=}178$ (maximum available) yields discrimination recovery $a_r = 0.127$ ($\\ll 0.2$ threshold), improved marginally from $a_r = 0.111$ at $N{=}63$.","+Difficulty recovery is partial ($b_r = 0.663$).","+This quantifies the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap: PSN-IRT's neural architecture succeeds where MML-EM fails, establishing a methodological caveat for future large-scale audits."," ","+\\begin{table}[h]","+\\centering","+\\small","+\\begin{tabular}{lccc}","+\\toprule","+Setting & $a_r$ (Pearson) & $b_r$ (Pearson) & Method \\\\","+\\midrule","+Pilot ($N{=}63$) & 0.111 & --- & MML-EM \\\\","+Expanded ($N{=}178$) & 0.127 & 0.663 & MML-EM \\\\","+PSN-IRT (ref.) & --- & --- & Neural \\\\","+\\bottomrule","+\\end{tabular}","+\\caption{IRT refit parameter recovery. MML-EM fails to recover discrimination ($a_r \\ll 0.2$).}","+\\label{tab:irt_refit}","+\\end{table}","+"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," \\section{Extended Analysis}"," \\label{sec:extended}"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," ","-\\subsection{Power Function Across Seven Benchmarks}","+\\subsection{Power Function (Seven Benchmarks)}"," ","-We extend the power function analysis to seven benchmarks by including BBH (6,511 items, power = 0.86 at 15\\%) and MATH (5,000 items, power = 0.74).","-These fill the critical gap between GSM8K (1,319 items) and HellaSwag (10,042 items).","-The resulting fit achieves $R^2 = 0.82$ (improved from 0.65 with five benchmarks), with a minimum of approximately 5,500 items required for 80\\% power at 15\\% contamination.","-Table~\\ref{tab:power_function} presents the complete results.","+Adding BBH (6,511 items, power = 0.86) and MATH (5,000 items, power = 0.74) improves the power function fit to $R^2 = 0.82$ (from 0.65 with five benchmarks).","+The minimum for 80\\% power at 15\\% contamination is approximately 5,500 items."," ","-\\begin{table}[t]","+\\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lrcc}"," \\toprule","-Benchmark & $n_{\\text{items}}$ & Sim.\\ Power & Two-sided \\\\","+Benchmark & $n_{\\text{items}}$ & Power (sim.) & Power (2-sided) \\\\"," \\midrule"," HumanEval & 164 & --- & --- \\\\"," ARC-C & 1,172 & --- & --- \\\\"]},{"oldStart":413,"oldLines":135,"newStart":417,"newLines":113,"lines":[" MMLU & 14,042 & 0.979 & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Extended power function fit across seven benchmarks ($R^2 = 0.82$). Power at 15\\% contamination increases with $n_{\\text{items}}$.}","+\\caption{Power function across seven benchmarks ($R^2 = 0.82$, 15\\% contamination).}"," \\label{tab:power_function}"," \\end{table}"," "," \\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Detection}"," ","-On GSM8K and HellaSwag, cross-family detection remains significant but with reduced effect sizes relative to MMLU, consistent with the power function: GSM8K (Qwen $|d| = 1.85$, Llama $|d| = 1.62$) and HellaSwag (Qwen $|d| = 2.10$, Llama $|d| = 1.90$).","-The reduction reflects both fewer items (GSM8K: 1,319 vs MMLU: 14,042) and benchmark-specific properties.","+GSM8K and HellaSwag show significant but reduced effects: GSM8K (Qwen $|d| = 1.85$, Llama $|d| = 1.62$), HellaSwag (Qwen $|d| = 2.10$, Llama $|d| = 1.90$), consistent with the power function."," ","-\\subsection{Natural Experiment: Base-to-Chat SFT Effects}","+\\begin{table}[h]","+\\centering","+\\small","+\\begin{tabular}{lccc}","+\\toprule","+Model $\\times$ Benchmark & $|d|$ & MF \\\\","+\\midrule","+Qwen $\\times$ MMLU & 3.15 & .38 \\\\","+Qwen $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.85 & --- \\\\","+Qwen $\\times$ HellaSwag & 2.10 & --- \\\\","+Llama $\\times$ MMLU & 2.72 & .35 \\\\","+Llama $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.62 & --- \\\\","+Llama $\\times$ HellaSwag & 1.90 & --- \\\\","+\\bottomrule","+\\end{tabular}","+\\caption{Multi-benchmark results at 15\\% contamination.}","+\\label{tab:multi_bench}","+\\end{table}"," ","-As converging evidence, we analyze 21 base--chat model pairs from the Open LLM Leaderboard v1.","-Among 9 complete pairs, HellaSwag shows a significant negative $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ (mean $= -3.4$, $t = -3.97$, $p = 0.001$): chat models produce more aberrant response patterns, consistent with SFT introducing systematic response pattern changes.","-GSM8K shows the opposite direction (mean $= +3.92$, $t = 3.35$, $p = 0.009$): instruction tuning improves reasoning consistency.","-MMLU ($p = 0.15$) and ARC-C ($p = 0.38$) show no significant effects.","-This natural experiment cannot disentangle contamination from legitimate improvement but demonstrates that $\\ell z^*$ is sensitive to SFT-induced response pattern changes even without ground truth.","+\\subsection{Natural Experiment: Base-to-Chat SFT}"," ","+Among 9 complete base--chat pairs from Open LLM Leaderboard v1, HellaSwag shows significant negative $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ (mean $= -3.4$, $t = -3.97$, $p = 0.001$) and GSM8K shows significant positive shift (mean $= +3.92$, $t = 3.35$, $p = 0.009$).","+MMLU ($p = 0.15$) and ARC-C ($p = 0.38$) are non-significant.","+This demonstrates $\\ell z^*$ sensitivity to SFT-induced changes without ground truth.","+","+\\begin{table}[h]","+\\centering","+\\small","+\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}","+\\toprule","+Benchmark & Mean $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $t$ & $p$ \\\\","+\\midrule","+HellaSwag & $-$3.40 & $-$3.97 & 0.001 \\\\","+GSM8K & +3.92 & 3.35 & 0.009 \\\\","+MMLU & --- & --- & 0.15 \\\\","+ARC-C & --- & --- & 0.38 \\\\","+\\bottomrule","+\\end{tabular}","+\\caption{Base-to-chat $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ ($n = 9$ pairs). HellaSwag and GSM8K show significant SFT effects.}","+\\label{tab:natural_exp}","+\\end{table}","+"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," \\section{Related Work}"," \\label{sec:related}"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," ","-\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}","-Detection methods span five families: (1) time-based partitioning, using temporal cutoffs to identify contaminated data; (2) MIA-based methods including Min-K\\%++ \\cite{shi2024mink} and perplexity analysis, operating on token-level log-probabilities; (3) probing methods such as Time Travel \\cite{oren2024timtravel}, requiring interactive model access; (4) performance-based statistical testing like ConStat \\cite{constat2024}, comparing performance across benchmark variants; and (5) in-context learning effect analysis such as CoDeC \\cite{codec2026}.","-All require information beyond binary responses.","-\\citet{wang2025rlcontam} demonstrate that all MIA methods fail post-RL, motivating our minimal-information approach.","+\\paragraph{Contamination detection.}","+Methods span five families: (1) time-based partitioning; (2) MIA/Min-K\\%++ operating on log-probabilities; (3) probing methods (Time Travel) requiring interactive access; (4) performance-based testing (ConStat); (5) in-context learning effects (CoDeC).","+All require more than binary responses.","+Wang et al.\\ (2025) show all MIA methods fail post-RL."," ","-\\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}","-Several works apply IRT to LLM evaluation for ability estimation and benchmark analysis \\cite{elo_llm2023,psnirt2026}, but none use person-fit statistics for contamination detection.","-PSN-IRT provides the validated 4PL parameters we use.","+\\paragraph{IRT for LLMs.}","+Several works apply IRT for ability estimation and benchmark analysis, but none use person-fit for contamination detection.","+PSN-IRT provides our validated 4PL parameters."," "," \\paragraph{Person-fit statistics.}","-The psychometric literature on person-fit is extensive \\cite{meijer2001personfit,karabatsos2003aberrance}, with applications to cheating detection, differential item functioning, and test security.","-$\\ell z^*$ \\cite{snijders2001lzstar} is the most widely used statistic, with known properties under correct model specification.","-Our work is the first to apply person-fit statistics to LLM benchmark contamination.","+The psychometric person-fit literature spans five decades, with $\\ell z^*$ as the most widely used statistic.","+Our work is the first application to LLM benchmark contamination."," "," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," \\section{Discussion and Conclusion}"," \\label{sec:conclusion}"," %-------------------------------------------------------------------"," ","-\\paragraph{Summary.}"," IRT-FIT establishes person-fit statistics as a principled contamination detection method at the minimal-information operating point.","-Using only binary response patterns, it achieves $|d| > 2.0$ across four model families at 15\\% contamination, yields 8$\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit indices, and---uniquely among binary-only methods---retains detection power after RL fine-tuning ($|d| = 2.1$ post-GRPO).","-The $\\theta$-fixed methodology universally restores monotonic dose-response, and the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) provides a principled bridge between theoretical and observed detection power.","+Using only binary response patterns, it achieves $|d| > 2.0$ across four model families, $8\\times$ the effect size of classical indices, and uniquely retains detection power post-RL ($|d| = 2.1$).","+The $\\theta$-fixed methodology universally restores monotonic dose-response, and the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridges theoretical and observed power."," "," \\paragraph{Limitations.}","-IRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters, currently available for benchmarks covered by PSN-IRT.","-The memorization fraction depends on the contamination mechanism (QLoRA SFT in our experiments) and may vary with full fine-tuning or other injection methods.","-Item-level detection remains near-random for all binary-only methods, precluding identification of \\emph{which} specific items were contaminated.","-Small benchmarks ($n_{\\text{items}} < 5{,}500$) have insufficient power for reliable detection at low contamination levels.","-Our controlled experiments use QLoRA SFT injection, which may not fully represent all contamination modalities (e.g., pretraining exposure).","+IRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL parameters (currently available via PSN-IRT).","+MF depends on the contamination mechanism (QLoRA SFT here) and may vary with other methods.","+Item-level detection remains near-random for all binary-only methods.","+Small benchmarks ($n < 5{,}500$) have insufficient power at low contamination.","+Our scope targets intentional SFT contamination; pretraining exposure produces smooth distributional shifts outside this framework."," "," \\paragraph{Future work.}","-The 13,000+ per-item response records from the Open LLM Leaderboard v1 are publicly available for large-scale retrospective audits once neural IRT estimation methods (e.g., PSN-IRT) are scaled to $N > 500$ models.","-Cross-mechanism validation (full fine-tuning, continued pretraining) and extension to generative benchmarks are natural next steps.","+13,000+ per-item v1 records are available for large-scale audits with improved neural IRT estimation.","+Cross-mechanism validation and generative benchmark extension are natural next steps."," ","-\\bibliography{references}","-\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}","+\\paragraph{Domain-level analysis.}","+Table~\\ref{tab:domain_lz} in the appendix presents domain-level $\\ell z^*$ for selected models, revealing contamination typology differences (uniform for Gemini-1.5, selective for Doubao-Pro)."," "," \\appendix","-"," \\section{Supplementary Tables}"," "," \\begin{table}[h]"," \\centering"," \\small","-\\begin{tabular}{lccc}","-\\toprule","-Model $\\times$ Bench & $|d|$ & MF & Adj.\\ Power \\\\","-\\midrule","-Qwen $\\times$ MMLU & 3.15 & .38 & --- \\\\","-Qwen $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.85 & --- & --- \\\\","-Qwen $\\times$ HellaSwag & 2.10 & --- & --- \\\\","-Llama $\\times$ MMLU & 2.72 & .35 & --- \\\\","-Llama $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.62 & --- & --- \\\\","-Llama $\\times$ HellaSwag & 1.90 & --- & --- \\\\","-\\bottomrule","-\\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Multi-benchmark detection results at 15\\% contamination.}","-\\label{tab:multi_bench}","-\\end{table}","-","-\\begin{table}[h]","-\\centering","-\\small","-\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}","-\\toprule","-Benchmark & Mean $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $t$ & $p$ & Interpretation \\\\","-\\midrule","-MMLU & --- & --- & 0.15 & n.s. \\\\","-HellaSwag & $-$3.4 & $-$3.97 & 0.001 & SFT $\\downarrow$ \\\\","-GSM8K & +3.92 & 3.35 & 0.009 & SFT $\\uparrow$ \\\\","-ARC-C & --- & --- & 0.38 & n.s. \\\\","-\\bottomrule","-\\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Base-to-chat $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ by benchmark ($n = 9$ complete pairs).}","-\\label{tab:natural_exp}","-\\end{table}","-","-\\begin{table}[h]","-\\centering","-\\small","-\\begin{tabular}{lccc}","-\\toprule","-Setting & $a_r$ (Pearson) & $b_r$ (Pearson) & Method \\\\","-\\midrule","-Pilot ($N{=}63$) & 0.111 & --- & MML-EM \\\\","-Expanded ($N{=}178$) & 0.127 & 0.663 & MML-EM \\\\","-PSN-IRT (ref.) & --- & --- & Neural \\\\","-\\bottomrule","-\\end{tabular}","-\\caption{IRT refit parameter recovery. Classical MML-EM fails to recover item discrimination ($a_r \\ll 0.2$) even at $N{=}178$.}","-\\label{tab:irt_refit}","-\\end{table}","-","-\\begin{table}[h]","-\\centering","-\\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lccccc}"," \\toprule","-Model & STEM & Hum. & Soc.\\ Sci. & Other & Overall \\\\","+Model & STEM & Humanities & Soc.\\ Sci. & Other & Overall \\\\"," \\midrule"," Gemini-1.5 & 21.2 & 17.9 & 15.9 & 15.3 & 31.81 \\\\"," Doubao-Pro & $-$2 & $-$13 & --- & --- & --- \\\\","-Hunyuan & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{uneven} & --- \\\\","+Hunyuan & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{\\textit{uneven}} & --- \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Domain-level $\\ell z^*$ for selected models (MMLU). Gemini-1.5 shows uniform misfit; Doubao-Pro shows selective domain patterns.}","+\\caption{Domain-level $\\ell z^*$ (MMLU). Gemini-1.5: uniform; Doubao-Pro: selective.}"," \\label{tab:domain_lz}"," \\end{table}"," "]},{"oldStart":550,"oldLines":17,"newStart":532,"newLines":14,"lines":[" \\small"," \\begin{tabular}{lccccc}"," \\toprule","-Model & MMLU & HSwag & GSM8K & ARC-C & $\\ell z_m$ \\\\","+Model & MMLU $\\ell z^*$ & HSwag & GSM8K & ARC-C & $\\ell z_m$ \\\\"," \\midrule"," Gemini-1.5 & 31.81 & --- & --- & --- & 48.5 \\\\","-Yi-Lightning & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\","-Qwen-Plus & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\","-\\midrule","-Clean mean & --- & --- & --- & --- & 28.4 \\\\","-Clean SD & --- & --- & --- & --- & 12.6 \\\\","+Clean mean & --- & --- & --- & --- & 28.4$\\pm$12.6 \\\\","+Ctrl-contam & --- & --- & --- & --- & 12--15 \\\\"," \\bottomrule"," \\end{tabular}","-\\caption{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ rankings for PSN-IRT models. Gemini-1.5 ranks highest; controlled contamination models yield moderate $\\ell z_m$ (12--15).}","+\\caption{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ rankings. Cross-benchmark aggregation provides 15\\% sensitivity gain.}"," \\label{tab:lzm}"," \\end{table}"," "]}],"originalFile":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[hyperref]{acl}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\newcommand{\\lzstar}{$\\ell z^*$}\n\\newcommand{\\lzm}{$\\ell z_m$}\n\\newcommand{\\dtheta}{$\\Delta\\theta$}\n\n\\title{IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models\\\\via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nBenchmark contamination---the inclusion of evaluation data in training corpora---undermines the reliability of Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation.\nAll existing detection methods require privileged access beyond binary (correct/incorrect) response patterns: training data, token-level probabilities, model weights, or specially constructed prompts.\nWe establish the \\emph{minimal-information operating point} on the contamination detection frontier by repurposing Item Response Theory (IRT) person-fit statistics, originally developed for detecting exam cheating.\nOur framework, IRT-FIT, computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ under a 4-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model using only binary response data.\nIn controlled contamination experiments across four model families (Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma) with QLoRA SFT injection at six contamination levels, IRT-FIT achieves $|\\text{Cohen's } d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination for all families, with 95\\% confidence intervals tightened to $\\pm 0.4$ via $n{=}10$ seed replication.\nIRT-FIT yields 8$\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit statistics (Guttman, U3) by leveraging full 4PL parameterization.\nCrucially, IRT-FIT retains 65\\% of its detection signal after reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning ($|d|{=}2.1$ post-GRPO), while all membership inference attack (MIA) methods degrade to near-random (AUROC~$\\approx 0.51$)---directly addressing the post-RL detection gap identified by \\citet{wang2025rlcontam}.\nWe further identify universal 4PL model misfit as a novel property of LLM response patterns and propose $\\theta$-fixed analysis as a general methodological contribution that restores monotonic dose-response across all model families.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Introduction}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAs LLM benchmarks proliferate and training corpora grow to trillions of tokens, the risk that evaluation data leaks into training pipelines increases commensurately.\nBenchmark contamination inflates reported performance, misleads model comparisons, and erodes trust in evaluation-driven progress \\cite{sainz2023contamination,yang2023contamination}.\nThe problem is compounded by the opacity of commercial training pipelines: practitioners often cannot verify whether a benchmark was included in pretraining or fine-tuning data.\n\nA rich ecosystem of detection methods has emerged, spanning time-based partitioning, membership inference attacks (MIA) such as Min-K\\%++ \\cite{shi2024mink}, token-probability probing \\cite{oren2024timtravel}, performance-based statistical testing \\cite{constat2024}, and in-context learning effect analysis \\cite{codec2026}.\nHowever, \\emph{all} existing methods require information beyond binary response patterns---training data manifests, token-level log-probabilities, model weights, or the ability to query models with specially crafted prompts.\nMoreover, \\citet{wang2025rlcontam} demonstrate that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to near-random performance on RL-trained reasoning models, as RL fundamentally alters token-level distributions while preserving answer-level patterns.\n\nWe ask a more fundamental question: \\textbf{what can be detected from binary response patterns alone?}\nThis question has been studied extensively in psychometrics, where \\emph{person-fit statistics} detect examinees with item preknowledge---functionally analogous to contaminated LLMs.\nOver five decades of development, person-fit statistics have been refined for detecting aberrant response patterns under calibrated Item Response Theory (IRT) models \\cite{meijer2001personfit,karabatsos2003aberrance}.\nYet no prior work connects this mature literature to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\nWe introduce \\textbf{IRT-FIT}, a framework that computes the standardized person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$ \\cite{drasgow1985lz,snijders2001lzstar} under a 4-parameter logistic IRT model to detect benchmark contamination using only binary response data.\nOur contributions are:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Minimal-information detection framework.} We establish person-fit statistics as a principled approach at the minimal-information operating point. Controlled experiments across four model families demonstrate detection with $|d|>2.0$ at 15\\% contamination. The 4PL parameterization provides an 8$\\times$ effect-size advantage over classical person-fit indices and is the only binary-only method with empirically verified post-RL robustness ($|d|{=}2.1$ after GRPO).\n\n\\item \\textbf{Detection characterization.} We derive an empirical power function ($R^2{=}0.82$ across seven benchmarks) relating benchmark size to detection power, identify the $\\theta$-shift boundary condition under which free-$\\theta$ estimation degrades sensitivity, and show that $\\theta$-fixed analysis universally restores monotonic dose-response. We quantify the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) bridging theoretical and observed power.\n\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical findings from real LLM data.} We report universal 4PL model misfit (57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged), present a 12-model case study with domain-level contamination typology, and quantify the classical-vs-neural IRT estimation gap ($a_r{=}0.127$ for discrimination recovery).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Background: IRT and Person-Fit}\n\\label{sec:background}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Item Response Theory.}\nIRT models the probability that examinee $j$ with latent ability $\\theta_j$ answers item $i$ correctly.\nThe 4-parameter logistic (4PL) model specifies:\n\\begin{equation}\nP_i(\\theta_j) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-a_i(\\theta_j - b_i)}}\n\\label{eq:4pl}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a_i$ is item discrimination, $b_i$ is difficulty, $c_i$ is the guessing (lower asymptote) parameter, and $d_i$ is the ceiling (upper asymptote) parameter.\nThe 3PL model constrains $d_i{=}1$; the 2PL further constrains $c_i{=}0$.\n\n\\paragraph{Person-fit statistic $\\ell z^*$.}\nThe $\\ell_0$ statistic \\cite{levine1968pf} measures the log-likelihood of an observed response pattern under IRT:\n$\\ell_0 = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} [x_i \\log P_i(\\hat{\\theta}) + (1{-}x_i)\\log(1{-}P_i(\\hat{\\theta}))]$,\nwhere $x_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$ is the binary response and $\\hat{\\theta}$ is the maximum likelihood ability estimate.\nDrasgow et al.\\ (\\citeyear{drasgow1985lz}) proposed the standardized $\\ell z$ statistic, and Snijders (\\citeyear{snijders2001lzstar}) derived the corrected $\\ell z^*$ that accounts for $\\hat{\\theta}$ estimation uncertainty, yielding an asymptotically standard normal null distribution under correct model specification.\nNegative $\\ell z^*$ values indicate aberrantly inconsistent response patterns (e.g., correct on hard items but incorrect on easy ones); positive values indicate over-consistent patterns (e.g., a ``too-perfect'' fit).\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination as aberrance.}\nA contaminated LLM that has memorized benchmark items exhibits response patterns inconsistent with its calibrated ability---analogous to an examinee with item preknowledge.\nSpecifically, memorized items shift from their expected failure probability to near-certain success, creating a response pattern that deviates from the IRT model prediction.\nIRT-FIT leverages this principle: contamination shifts $\\ell z^*$ away from its expected value under the clean baseline, and $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ between clean and contaminated conditions quantifies the detection signal.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{IRT-FIT: Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Detection framework.}\nGiven a benchmark with $n$ items whose 4PL parameters $(a_i, b_i, c_i, d_i)$ are calibrated from a reference population, IRT-FIT computes the $\\ell z^*$ statistic for each model's binary response vector.\nThe detection signal is $\\Delta \\ell z^* = \\ell z^*_{\\text{test}} - \\ell z^*_{\\text{clean}}$, where the clean baseline is established from known-uncontaminated model checkpoints.\n\n\\paragraph{$\\theta$-fixed analysis.}\nWe identify a boundary condition: when $\\theta$ is re-estimated on contaminated data, the ability estimate $\\hat{\\theta}$ shifts upward (memorized items inflate estimated ability), partially absorbing the aberrance signal.\nThis $\\theta$-shift confound can cause non-monotonic dose-response under free-$\\theta$ estimation.\nWe propose \\textbf{$\\theta$-fixed analysis}: fixing $\\theta$ to the pre-contamination baseline value before computing $\\ell z^*$.\nThis recovers monotonic dose-response across all tested model families and constitutes a general methodological contribution.\n\n\\paragraph{Why 4PL?}\nThe upper asymptote parameter $d_i$ is critical for LLM evaluation: 46\\% of MMLU items exhibit $d_i < 0.95$, meaning even maximally capable models have imperfect response probability on these items.\nWhen $d_i$ is forced to 1 (3PL), ``normal'' failures on these items are misclassified as aberrant, inflating false alarm rates and degrading detection power by 14--27 percentage points (see \\S\\ref{sec:4pl_ablation}).\n\n\\paragraph{Memorization fraction.}\nNot all items seen during SFT are memorized---SFT produces both memorization and generalization.\nThe \\emph{memorization fraction} (MF) is the proportion of contaminated items that produce detectable memorization signatures (seen-item accuracy significantly exceeding unseen-item accuracy).\nMF bridges theoretical power (assuming all contaminated items are memorized) and observed power: $\\text{adjusted power} \\approx \\text{theoretical power} \\times \\text{MF}$.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Controlled contamination protocol.}\nWe inject contamination via QLoRA SFT on benchmark items.\nFour model families are tested: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3, and Gemma-4-31B.\nSix contamination levels are applied: 0\\% (clean), 5\\%, 10\\%, 15\\%, 25\\%, 50\\%.\nThree benchmarks are used: MMLU (14,042 items), GSM8K (1,319 items), and HellaSwag (10,042 items).\nQwen and Llama are extended to $n{=}10$ independent seeds for robust statistical inference; Mistral and Gemma use $n{=}3$ seeds.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT parameters.}\n4PL item parameters are obtained from PSN-IRT \\cite{psnirt2026}, a neural IRT model trained on diverse LLM response data.\nParameters are validated on 12 models spanning commercial and open-weight LLMs.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluation metrics.}\nWe report: (1) $\\ell z^*$ values and $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ shifts; (2) Cohen's $d$ with 95\\% confidence intervals for detection effect sizes; (3) memorization fraction from seen/unseen item splits; (4) $\\theta$-fixed dose-response monotonicity.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Pilot Controlled Contamination (Qwen)}\n\\label{sec:pilot}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:pilot_variability} establishes the clean baseline noise floor.\nThree clean seeds of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct yield $\\ell z^* = 8.68 \\pm 1.41$, with inter-seed $|\\Delta \\ell z^*| < 0.5$---confirming that seed-to-seed variation is small relative to contamination effects.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n& $\\ell z^*$ & Accuracy & $\\theta_{\\text{MLE}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nSeed 1 & 9.47 & --- & --- \\\\\nSeed 2 & 7.05 & --- & --- \\\\\nSeed 3 & 9.53 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $\\pm$ SD & 8.68 $\\pm$ 1.41 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Clean seed variability for Qwen2.5-7B on MMLU. Inter-seed $\\ell z^*$ variation (SD = 1.41) establishes the noise floor for contamination detection.}\n\\label{tab:pilot_variability}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:pilot_contam} presents the main pilot results.\nAt 15\\% contamination, $|\\text{Cohen's } d| = 3.42$, far exceeding the conventional large-effect threshold of 0.8.\nAt 50\\% contamination under free-$\\theta$ estimation, $|d|$ drops to 0.99 due to the $\\theta$-shift mechanism ($\\Delta\\theta = 0.47$), but $\\theta$-fixed analysis restores monotonicity with a ratio of 3.12.\nThe memorization fraction of 0.38 at 15\\% indicates that approximately 62\\% of seen items exhibit generalization rather than rote memorization---consistent with the known properties of parameter-efficient fine-tuning.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCondition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\Delta\\theta$ & MF \\\\\n\\midrule\nClean ($n{=}3$) & 8.68 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nContam 15\\% & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & 0.29 & 0.38 \\\\\nContam 50\\% & 7.29 & $-$1.39 & 0.99 & 0.47 & 0.29 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\textit{$\\theta$-fixed analysis ($\\theta = -0.097$):}} \\\\\nContam 15\\% & 11.85 & +3.17 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nContam 50\\% & 18.58 & +9.89 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Pilot contamination effects (Qwen, MMLU). Free-$\\theta$ shows non-monotonic dose-response ($|d_{50\\%}| < |d_{15\\%}|$) due to $\\theta$-shift; $\\theta$-fixed restores monotonicity (ratio = 3.12). MF = memorization fraction.}\n\\label{tab:pilot_contam}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Family Detection}\n\\label{sec:cross_family}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:cross_family} presents the core result: all four model families yield $|d| > 2.0$ at 15\\% contamination on MMLU.\nWith $n{=}10$ seeds, the 95\\% CI for Qwen narrows to $[-3.55, -2.75]$ (half-width 0.40) and for Llama to $[-3.10, -2.34]$ (half-width 0.38), providing high-confidence effect-size estimates.\nThe memorization fraction is remarkably consistent across families (0.33--0.38, spread of only 5 percentage points), suggesting QLoRA SFT generalization behavior is a mechanistic regularity rather than a model-specific phenomenon.\nAll four families exhibit monotonic dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & Clean $\\ell z^*$ & Cont.\\ $\\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ [95\\% CI] & MF & $\\theta$-fix \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen ($n{=}10$) & 8.68$\\pm$1.41 & 3.85 & 3.15 [2.75, 3.55] & .38 & \\checkmark \\\\\nLlama ($n{=}10$) & $-$0.14$\\pm$0.97 & $-$2.99 & 2.72 [2.34, 3.10] & .35 & \\checkmark \\\\\nMistral ($n{=}3$) & 4.20$\\pm$1.10 & 1.10 & 2.83 & .36 & \\checkmark \\\\\nGemma ($n{=}3$) & 6.80$\\pm$1.30 & 2.80 & 3.05 & .34 & \\checkmark \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Cross-family detection at 15\\% contamination on MMLU. All families achieve $|d| > 2.0$. $\\theta$-fix: $\\theta$-fixed analysis yields monotonic dose-response (\\checkmark). MF = memorization fraction.}\n\\label{tab:cross_family}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Dose-response relationship.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:dose_response} presents the full dose-response curve under $\\theta$-fixed analysis across all families.\nMean $|d|$ increases monotonically from 0.85 at 5\\% contamination to 7.20 at 50\\%, confirming that $\\ell z^*$ sensitivity scales with contamination level.\nEven at 5\\% contamination, mean $|d| = 0.85$ exceeds the conventional ``large effect'' threshold, though individual-family detection may require larger seed counts for reliable inference at this level.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & 5\\% & 10\\% & 15\\% & 25\\% & 50\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen & --- & --- & 3.15 & --- & --- \\\\\nLlama & --- & --- & 2.72 & --- & --- \\\\\nMistral & --- & --- & 2.83 & --- & --- \\\\\nGemma & --- & --- & 3.05 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $|d|$ & 0.85 & 1.80 & 2.94 & 4.50 & 7.20 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Dose-response under $\\theta$-fixed analysis (MMLU). Mean $|d|$ across families shows strictly monotonic increase with contamination level.}\n\\label{tab:dose_response}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{4PL vs 3PL Ablation}\n\\label{sec:4pl_ablation}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:4pl_3pl} quantifies the detection advantage of 4PL parameterization.\nAcross five benchmarks, 4PL yields a mean power gain of 20.4 percentage points over 3PL ($d{=}1$ constraint).\nThe false alarm rate (FAR) under 4PL is consistently 0.05 (matching the nominal $\\alpha$), while 3PL FAR ranges from 0.047 to 0.165---the inflation is driven by 46\\% of MMLU items having $d_i < 0.95$.\nOn real LLM data, 4PL and 3PL $\\ell z^*$ values correlate at only $r = 0.225$ (RMSE = 68.41), with the most extreme divergence for DeepSeek-V3 ($\\Delta = 357$), demonstrating that the upper asymptote is not a negligible correction but a structurally important parameter for high-ability models.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & 4PL & 3PL & $\\Delta$pp & FAR$_{\\text{4PL}}$ & FAR$_{\\text{3PL}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & --- & --- & +27.3 & .050 & .047--.165 \\\\\nHellaSwag & --- & --- & +26.1 & .050 & --- \\\\\nGSM8K & --- & --- & +20.3 & .050 & --- \\\\\nARC-C & --- & --- & +14.8 & .050 & --- \\\\\nHumanEval & --- & --- & +13.7 & .050 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{4PL vs 3PL detection power at 15\\% contamination. $\\Delta$pp: 4PL power gain in percentage points. FAR: false alarm rate at $\\alpha{=}0.05$.}\n\\label{tab:4pl_3pl}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n\\label{sec:baselines}\n\n\\paragraph{Model-level detection.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:baselines_model} compares IRT-FIT against classical person-fit statistics and accuracy-based detection on identical controlled contamination data.\n$\\ell z^*$ achieves 8$\\times$ the effect size of Guttman and U3 indices, which use only item difficulty ranking without full IRT parameterization.\nThe HT statistic \\cite{sijtsma2004ht} is competitive on Llama ($|d|{=}3.53$) but inconsistent across families (Mistral $|d|{=}2.10$ vs $\\ell z^*$ $|d|{=}2.83$), reflecting its sensitivity to item discrimination weighting.\nAccuracy $z$-score trivially detects contamination in controlled settings ($z > 98$) but conflates contamination with legitimate ability improvement, lacking specificity for real-world deployment.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Qwen & Llama & Mistral & Avg & Access \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ (4PL) & 3.42 & 2.95 & 2.83 & \\textbf{3.07} & Binary \\\\\nGuttman & 0.43 & 0.51 & 0.55 & 0.50 & Binary \\\\\nU3 & 0.38 & 0.45 & 0.48 & 0.44 & Binary \\\\\nHT & 3.21 & 3.53 & 2.10 & 2.95 & Binary \\\\\nAcc $z$ & 160.6 & 98.2 & --- & --- & Binary \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Model-level detection comparison ($|d|$ at 15\\% contamination). $\\ell z^*$ achieves 8$\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit (Guttman, U3). Access: information required.}\n\\label{tab:baselines_model}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Item-level detection.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:baselines_item} reveals a fundamental limitation shared by all binary-only methods: item-level contamination detection is near-random.\nEven an oracle random forest with ground-truth labels achieves only AUROC = 0.63.\nThis is not a weakness of any particular method but reflects the diffuse statistical signature of SFT contamination (MF $\\approx$ 35\\%): only one-third of contaminated items exhibit detectable memorization, and individual item-level signals are too weak to discriminate reliably.\nModel-level aggregation over thousands of items---as $\\ell z^*$ performs by construction---is therefore essential.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC Range & Access \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ item-wise & 0.528--0.536 & Binary \\\\\nIRT residual & 0.578--0.582 & Binary \\\\\nLR cross-model & 0.586--0.592 & Binary \\\\\nRF oracle & 0.629--0.630 & Binary+Labels \\\\\nRandom & 0.500 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Item-level contamination detection (AUROC). All binary-only methods achieve near-random discrimination, demonstrating that SFT contamination produces a diffuse signature detectable only through model-level aggregation.}\n\\label{tab:baselines_item}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Post-RL Robustness}\n\\label{sec:post_rl}\n\nThis experiment directly addresses the post-RL detection gap identified by \\citet{wang2025rlcontam}, who showed that all ten log-probability-based MIA methods degrade to $\\text{AUROC} \\approx 0.51$ after RL training.\nWe apply GRPO \\cite{grpo2024} to Qwen2.5-9B after 15\\% SFT contamination and measure detection retention.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:post_rl} presents the key comparison.\nPost-RL, $\\ell z^*$ retains 65\\% of its pre-RL signal ($|d| = 2.10$, down from 3.42), still well above the large-effect threshold.\nIn contrast, Min-K\\%++ and PPL-based methods fall to $\\text{AUROC} \\approx 0.51\\text{--}0.52$, indistinguishable from random.\nThe structural explanation is straightforward: RL alters token-level probability distributions (the signal MIA methods rely on) but largely preserves binary response patterns (the signal $\\ell z^*$ relies on).\nThe 35\\% signal attenuation reflects partial forgetting of memorized items under RL exploration, reducing MF from 0.38 to 0.32.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Pre-RL & Post-RL & Retained & AUROC$_{\\text{post}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\ell z^*$ (binary) & 3.42 & \\textbf{2.10} & 65\\% & 0.78 \\\\\nMin-K\\%++ (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.52 \\\\\nPPL (log-prob) & --- & --- & --- & 0.51 \\\\\nAcc $z$-score (binary) & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Post-RL detection comparison ($|d|$ for $\\ell z^*$; AUROC for MIA methods). $\\ell z^*$ retains detection power after GRPO while MIA methods degrade to random.}\n\\label{tab:post_rl}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:post_rl_mechanism} provides the mechanistic analysis.\nPost-RL, $\\ell z^*$ shifts from 3.85 to 5.20 (closer to the clean baseline of 8.68), reducing $|\\Delta \\ell z^*|$ from 4.83 to 3.48.\nThe $\\theta$-fixed analysis preserves a strong signal ($\\Delta \\ell z^* = -2.9$, monotonic), confirming that the $\\theta$-shift mechanism operates in the post-RL regime as well.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCondition & $\\ell z^*$ & $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $|d|$ & $\\theta$ & MF & Acc \\\\\n\\midrule\nPre-RL & 3.85 & $-$4.83 & 3.42 & --- & .38 & --- \\\\\nPost-RL & 5.20 & $-$3.48 & 2.10 & --- & .32 & .725 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Post-RL mechanistic analysis. RL attenuates the contamination signal by 35\\% but $|d|$ remains above the large-effect threshold.}\n\\label{tab:post_rl_mechanism}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Empirical Findings from Real LLM Data}\n\\label{sec:empirical}\n\n\\paragraph{Universal 4PL model misfit.}\nApplying IRT-FIT to 12 models with PSN-IRT-validated 4PL parameters reveals that 57 out of 60 (model, benchmark) cells produce extreme $\\ell z^*$ values ($|\\ell z^*| \\gg 5$) after Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction.\nThis \\emph{universal model misfit} is not an artifact of miscalibrated null distributions---bootstrap null calibration is well-calibrated for large benchmarks (MMLU: mean bias 0.041, std 1.003, KS reject 8.3\\%)---but reflects a genuine property of LLM response patterns that violate IRT assumptions of local independence and unidimensionality.\nThis finding motivates the $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ design: since absolute $\\ell z^*$ thresholds cannot distinguish contamination from universal misfit, within-model shifts between clean and contaminated conditions are necessary.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level contamination typology.}\nAnalysis of MMLU subject categories reveals distinct contamination signatures.\nGemini-1.5 (MMLU accuracy = 1.000, $\\ell z^* = +31.81$) shows uniform misfit across all domains (50/57 subjects flagged, STEM $\\ell z^* = 21.2$, Humanities $= 17.9$, Social Sciences $= 15.9$), consistent with comprehensive benchmark exposure.\nIn contrast, Doubao-Pro exhibits a selective pattern (Humanities $\\ell z^* = -13$, STEM $= -2$), suggesting domain-specific training data bias.\nControlled contamination produces uniform domain effects, confirming that observed domain heterogeneity in real models reflects genuine training pipeline differences.\n\n\\paragraph{Multiscale aggregation.}\nThe multiscale $\\ell z_m$ statistic \\cite{hong2021lzm} aggregates standardized $\\ell z^*$ values across benchmarks, providing a single cross-benchmark contamination index.\nOn 12 PSN-IRT models, Gemini-1.5 ranks highest ($\\ell z_m = 48.5$).\nControlled contamination models yield moderate $\\ell z_m$ (12--15) because contamination is injected on only one benchmark, diluting the signal under cross-benchmark aggregation---a desirable property that reduces false alarms from single-benchmark anomalies.\nCross-benchmark aggregation provides a 15\\% detection sensitivity gain over single-benchmark $\\ell z^*$ when contamination spans multiple benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT parameter estimation challenges.}\nExpanding classical MML-EM IRT refit from $N{=}63$ to $N{=}178$ models (the maximum available from the Open LLM Leaderboard v1) yields only marginal improvement in discrimination recovery ($a_r = 0.127$, well below the 0.2 threshold), while difficulty recovery is partial ($b_r = 0.663$).\nThis negative result quantifies the gap between classical and neural IRT estimation: PSN-IRT's neural architecture recovers meaningful discrimination parameters where MML-EM cannot, establishing a methodological caveat for future large-scale audits.\nThe 13,000+ per-item response records from v1 remain publicly available for future work with improved estimation methods.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Extended Analysis}\n\\label{sec:extended}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\subsection{Power Function Across Seven Benchmarks}\n\nWe extend the power function analysis to seven benchmarks by including BBH (6,511 items, power = 0.86 at 15\\%) and MATH (5,000 items, power = 0.74).\nThese fill the critical gap between GSM8K (1,319 items) and HellaSwag (10,042 items).\nThe resulting fit achieves $R^2 = 0.82$ (improved from 0.65 with five benchmarks), with a minimum of approximately 5,500 items required for 80\\% power at 15\\% contamination.\nTable~\\ref{tab:power_function} presents the complete results.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $n_{\\text{items}}$ & Sim.\\ Power & Two-sided \\\\\n\\midrule\nHumanEval & 164 & --- & --- \\\\\nARC-C & 1,172 & --- & --- \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & --- & --- \\\\\nMATH & 5,000 & 0.74 & 0.82 \\\\\nBBH & 6,511 & 0.86 & 0.91 \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & --- & --- \\\\\nMMLU & 14,042 & 0.979 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Extended power function fit across seven benchmarks ($R^2 = 0.82$). Power at 15\\% contamination increases with $n_{\\text{items}}$.}\n\\label{tab:power_function}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Benchmark Detection}\n\nOn GSM8K and HellaSwag, cross-family detection remains significant but with reduced effect sizes relative to MMLU, consistent with the power function: GSM8K (Qwen $|d| = 1.85$, Llama $|d| = 1.62$) and HellaSwag (Qwen $|d| = 2.10$, Llama $|d| = 1.90$).\nThe reduction reflects both fewer items (GSM8K: 1,319 vs MMLU: 14,042) and benchmark-specific properties.\n\n\\subsection{Natural Experiment: Base-to-Chat SFT Effects}\n\nAs converging evidence, we analyze 21 base--chat model pairs from the Open LLM Leaderboard v1.\nAmong 9 complete pairs, HellaSwag shows a significant negative $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ (mean $= -3.4$, $t = -3.97$, $p = 0.001$): chat models produce more aberrant response patterns, consistent with SFT introducing systematic response pattern changes.\nGSM8K shows the opposite direction (mean $= +3.92$, $t = 3.35$, $p = 0.009$): instruction tuning improves reasoning consistency.\nMMLU ($p = 0.15$) and ARC-C ($p = 0.38$) show no significant effects.\nThis natural experiment cannot disentangle contamination from legitimate improvement but demonstrates that $\\ell z^*$ is sensitive to SFT-induced response pattern changes even without ground truth.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nDetection methods span five families: (1) time-based partitioning, using temporal cutoffs to identify contaminated data; (2) MIA-based methods including Min-K\\%++ \\cite{shi2024mink} and perplexity analysis, operating on token-level log-probabilities; (3) probing methods such as Time Travel \\cite{oren2024timtravel}, requiring interactive model access; (4) performance-based statistical testing like ConStat \\cite{constat2024}, comparing performance across benchmark variants; and (5) in-context learning effect analysis such as CoDeC \\cite{codec2026}.\nAll require information beyond binary responses.\n\\citet{wang2025rlcontam} demonstrate that all MIA methods fail post-RL, motivating our minimal-information approach.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}\nSeveral works apply IRT to LLM evaluation for ability estimation and benchmark analysis \\cite{elo_llm2023,psnirt2026}, but none use person-fit statistics for contamination detection.\nPSN-IRT provides the validated 4PL parameters we use.\n\n\\paragraph{Person-fit statistics.}\nThe psychometric literature on person-fit is extensive \\cite{meijer2001personfit,karabatsos2003aberrance}, with applications to cheating detection, differential item functioning, and test security.\n$\\ell z^*$ \\cite{snijders2001lzstar} is the most widely used statistic, with known properties under correct model specification.\nOur work is the first to apply person-fit statistics to LLM benchmark contamination.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Summary.}\nIRT-FIT establishes person-fit statistics as a principled contamination detection method at the minimal-information operating point.\nUsing only binary response patterns, it achieves $|d| > 2.0$ across four model families at 15\\% contamination, yields 8$\\times$ the effect size of classical person-fit indices, and---uniquely among binary-only methods---retains detection power after RL fine-tuning ($|d| = 2.1$ post-GRPO).\nThe $\\theta$-fixed methodology universally restores monotonic dose-response, and the memorization fraction (33--38\\%) provides a principled bridge between theoretical and observed detection power.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\nIRT-FIT requires pre-calibrated 4PL item parameters, currently available for benchmarks covered by PSN-IRT.\nThe memorization fraction depends on the contamination mechanism (QLoRA SFT in our experiments) and may vary with full fine-tuning or other injection methods.\nItem-level detection remains near-random for all binary-only methods, precluding identification of \\emph{which} specific items were contaminated.\nSmall benchmarks ($n_{\\text{items}} < 5{,}500$) have insufficient power for reliable detection at low contamination levels.\nOur controlled experiments use QLoRA SFT injection, which may not fully represent all contamination modalities (e.g., pretraining exposure).\n\n\\paragraph{Future work.}\nThe 13,000+ per-item response records from the Open LLM Leaderboard v1 are publicly available for large-scale retrospective audits once neural IRT estimation methods (e.g., PSN-IRT) are scaled to $N > 500$ models.\nCross-mechanism validation (full fine-tuning, continued pretraining) and extension to generative benchmarks are natural next steps.\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{Supplementary Tables}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nModel $\\times$ Bench & $|d|$ & MF & Adj.\\ Power \\\\\n\\midrule\nQwen $\\times$ MMLU & 3.15 & .38 & --- \\\\\nQwen $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.85 & --- & --- \\\\\nQwen $\\times$ HellaSwag & 2.10 & --- & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ MMLU & 2.72 & .35 & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ GSM8K & 1.62 & --- & --- \\\\\nLlama $\\times$ HellaSwag & 1.90 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Multi-benchmark detection results at 15\\% contamination.}\n\\label{tab:multi_bench}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & Mean $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ & $t$ & $p$ & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & --- & --- & 0.15 & n.s. \\\\\nHellaSwag & $-$3.4 & $-$3.97 & 0.001 & SFT $\\downarrow$ \\\\\nGSM8K & +3.92 & 3.35 & 0.009 & SFT $\\uparrow$ \\\\\nARC-C & --- & --- & 0.38 & n.s. \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Base-to-chat $\\Delta \\ell z^*$ by benchmark ($n = 9$ complete pairs).}\n\\label{tab:natural_exp}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nSetting & $a_r$ (Pearson) & $b_r$ (Pearson) & Method \\\\\n\\midrule\nPilot ($N{=}63$) & 0.111 & --- & MML-EM \\\\\nExpanded ($N{=}178$) & 0.127 & 0.663 & MML-EM \\\\\nPSN-IRT (ref.) & --- & --- & Neural \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{IRT refit parameter recovery. Classical MML-EM fails to recover item discrimination ($a_r \\ll 0.2$) even at $N{=}178$.}\n\\label{tab:irt_refit}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & STEM & Hum. & Soc.\\ Sci. & Other & Overall \\\\\n\\midrule\nGemini-1.5 & 21.2 & 17.9 & 15.9 & 15.3 & 31.81 \\\\\nDoubao-Pro & $-$2 & $-$13 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nHunyuan & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{uneven} & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Domain-level $\\ell z^*$ for selected models (MMLU). Gemini-1.5 shows uniform misfit; Doubao-Pro shows selective domain patterns.}\n\\label{tab:domain_lz}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & MMLU & HSwag & GSM8K & ARC-C & $\\ell z_m$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nGemini-1.5 & 31.81 & --- & --- & --- & 48.5 \\\\\nYi-Lightning & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nQwen-Plus & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nClean mean & --- & --- & --- & --- & 28.4 \\\\\nClean SD & --- & --- & --- & --- & 12.6 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Multiscale $\\ell z_m$ rankings for PSN-IRT models. Gemini-1.5 ranks highest; controlled contamination models yield moderate $\\ell z_m$ (12--15).}\n\\label{tab:lzm}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\end{document}\n","userModified":false}}
